// who the famous people were, in a few lines: the first is their life and death, the rest are curious facts (keys are the Bulgarian names)
export const BIO_EN: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "Имхотеп": ["An Egyptian sage and adviser to the pharaoh Djoser. He built the first pyramid in the world, the Step Pyramid at Saqqara. He was a healer as well as a builder, and nobody knows when or how he died.", "He is the first architect in history whose name we know.", "About 2,000 years after his death, Egyptians were praying to him as a god of healing."],
  "Хамурапи": ["A king of Babylon who united almost all of Mesopotamia. He had 282 laws carved on a tall black stone so that everyone could know what was just. He died old and ill after about 42 years on the throne.", "The stone with his laws was found in 1901 and now stands in the Louvre in Paris.", "The rule “an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth” comes from it."],
  "Моисей": ["According to the Bible, he led the Hebrews out of slavery in Egypt and through the desert for 40 years. On Mount Sinai he received the Ten Commandments. He died at 120 on Mount Nebo, where he saw the promised land but never entered it.", "As a baby his mother put him in a basket on the Nile, and the pharaoh's daughter found him.", "Michelangelo's statue of him has horns because of a mistranslation of the word for “rays”."],
  "Нефертити": ["An Egyptian queen, wife of the pharaoh Akhenaten. Together they replaced the many old gods with one, the sun disc Aten. Nobody knows how or when she died, and her tomb has never been found.", "Her name means “the beautiful one has come”.", "Her famous painted bust was found by German archaeologists in 1912 and is now in Berlin."],
  "Тутанкамон": ["He became pharaoh at about 9 and ruled Egypt for only about ten years. He died young, at about 18 or 19, probably of illness after a broken leg. He became world famous when Howard Carter found his almost untouched tomb in 1922.", "His golden mask weighs about 10 kilograms.", "His tomb held more than 130 walking sticks: he had a bad foot and needed them."],
  "Рамзес II": ["The mightiest pharaoh of Egypt, who ruled for 66 years. He built huge temples, among them Abu Simbel, and fought the battle of Kadesh against the Hittites. He died at about 90, having outlived many of his children.", "His peace treaty with the Hittites is the oldest known in the world; a copy hangs at the United Nations.", "He had more than 100 children."],
  "Цар Давид": ["According to the Bible, a shepherd boy who beat the giant Goliath with a single stone from a sling. He later became king of Israel and made Jerusalem his capital. He died at 70 and left the throne to his son Solomon.", "Tradition says he wrote many of the Psalms.", "Michelangelo's marble “David” is over 5 metres tall."],
  "Цар Соломон": ["Son of David and king of Israel, famous for his wisdom and wealth. He built the first temple in Jerusalem. He died after about 40 years on the throne, and after him the kingdom split in two.", "In the famous “judgement of Solomon”, two women claimed the same baby; he offered to cut it in half and knew the true mother because she gave it up to save it.", "The Queen of Sheba came to test his wisdom."],
  "Солон": ["A lawmaker of Athens and one of the Seven Sages. He cancelled the debts of the poor and banned selling people into slavery for debt. He laid the foundations of Athenian democracy and died at about 80.", "After giving his laws he travelled for 10 years so that nobody could make him change them.", "He told the rich king Croesus: “Call no man happy until he is dead.”"],
  "Сафо": ["A poet from the island of Lesbos who sang her verses to the lyre. She wrote about love, friendship and beauty. How she died is unknown; the legend that she leapt from a cliff for love is probably made up.", "Plato called her “the tenth Muse”.", "Of her nine books only one whole poem has survived; the rest are scraps of papyrus."],
  "Езоп": ["A teller of fables who, legend says, was a slave on the island of Samos and was later freed. The animals in his fables, the fox, the tortoise, the hare, teach people wisdom. Legend says he was killed at Delphi, thrown from a cliff.", "The phrase “sour grapes” comes from his fable of the fox who cannot reach the grapes.", "“The Tortoise and the Hare” is his."],
  "Питагор": ["Born on the island of Samos, he founded a school in Croton in southern Italy. His followers believed the world is made of numbers. He died at about 75; legend says he was killed because he would not flee across a field of beans.", "His theorem a² + b² = c² was known to the Babylonians even earlier.", "He was the first to find that pleasing musical notes are simple ratios of numbers."],
  "Буда": ["Prince Siddhartha Gautama, born in Lumbini in today's Nepal. At 29 he left his palace after seeing old age, sickness and death. Under the Bodhi tree he reached enlightenment, and for 45 years he taught people how to be free of suffering. He died at 80 in Kushinagar.", "“Buddha” means “the awakened one”.", "The fat, laughing “Buddha” of the figurines is someone else: the Chinese monk Budai."],
  "Конфуций": ["A teacher from the state of Lu in China. He taught respect for parents, learning all your life and honest government. He died at 72, and his students wrote his thoughts down in the Analects.", "His rule: “Do not do to others what you do not want done to yourself.”", "His family tree has been followed for over 80 generations, the longest in the world."],
  "Есхил": ["An Athenian called “the father of tragedy”. He fought the Persians at Marathon and later wrote about 90 plays, of which 7 survive. He died at 69 in Sicily.", "Legend says an eagle dropped a tortoise on his bald head, taking it for a stone.", "His grave says only that he fought at Marathon; his plays are not mentioned."],
  "Софокъл": ["An Athenian playwright, author of “Oedipus the King” and “Antigone”. He wrote 123 plays, 7 of which survive, and won first prize about 24 times. He died at 91, writing to the very end.", "He was the first to put a third actor on stage.", "When his sons tried to have him declared unfit, he read from his new play in court and won."],
  "Перикъл": ["The leader of Athens in its golden age. He strengthened democracy and built the Parthenon on the Acropolis. He died at 66 of the plague that struck Athens during the war with Sparta.", "He is always shown in a helmet, said to hide his unusually long head.", "His speech for those who fell in the war is still counted among the greatest speeches about democracy."],
  "Херодот": ["Born in Halicarnassus, today's Bodrum in Turkey. He travelled to Egypt, Babylon and the Black Sea and wrote down everything he saw in his “Histories”, the story of the wars between Greeks and Persians. He died at about 59, probably in southern Italy.", "Cicero called him “the father of history”.", "Some also call him “the father of lies”: he wrote, for example, of ants in India as big as foxes that dig for gold."],
  "Сократ": ["An Athenian, son of a stonemason, who never wrote a single line. He walked the market square asking questions until people found the truth by themselves. He was sentenced to death for “corrupting the youth”, and at 71 he drank a cup of poison hemlock.", "His saying: “I know that I know nothing.”", "He went barefoot and was famous for his plain face, yet the young people of Athens adored him."],
  "Хипократ": ["A physician from the island of Kos, called “the father of medicine”. He was the first to teach that illnesses have natural causes and are not punishments from the gods. He died at a great age in Larissa; legend says he lived past 90.", "Doctors still take the “Hippocratic Oath” today.", "He described illnesses so precisely that some still carry his name."],
  "Демокрит": ["Born in Abdera in Thrace, not far from today's Bulgarian border. He was the first to say that everything is made of tiny particles that cannot be split: atoms. He died at about 90, cheerful to the end.", "“Atom” means “uncuttable” in Greek.", "He is called “the laughing philosopher” because he joked about human foolishness."],
  "Платон": ["An Athenian, student of Socrates. He founded the Academy, a school that lasted about 900 years. He wrote dialogues in which Socrates talks with people, and died at about 80 in Athens.", "“Plato” is a nickname meaning “the broad one”, probably for his wrestler's shoulders.", "He was the first to tell of the lost island of Atlantis."],
  "Аристотел": ["Born in Stagira, a student of Plato and teacher of Alexander the Great. He wrote about almost everything: logic, physics, animals, poetry and the state. He died at 62 in Chalcis, after leaving Athens.", "He was the first to sort animals into groups and described more than 500 kinds.", "He taught while walking about, so his school is called “peripatetic”, meaning “walking around”."],
  "Александър Македонски": ["Born in Pella in Macedonia, a student of Aristotle. He became king at 20 and in 11 years conquered the Persian Empire all the way to India without losing a battle. He died at 32 in Babylon of a fever.", "He founded about 20 cities named Alexandria; the most famous is in Egypt.", "As a boy he tamed the horse Bucephalus by noticing it was afraid of its own shadow and turning it towards the sun."],
  "Евклид": ["A mathematician from Alexandria about whose life almost nothing is known. His book “Elements” gathers all the geometry of his time in 13 volumes. It was used to teach mathematics for more than 2,000 years.", "When King Ptolemy asked for an easier way to learn geometry, he answered: “There is no royal road to geometry.”", "After the Bible, “Elements” may be the most printed book in history."],
  "Ашока": ["An emperor of the Maurya dynasty who ruled almost all of India. After the bloody war with Kalinga he repented, became a Buddhist and preached non-violence. He had his laws carved on stone pillars across the empire and died at about 72.", "The lions from his pillar at Sarnath are the national emblem of India.", "The wheel from his pillars is in the middle of the Indian flag."],
  "Архимед": ["A scientist from Syracuse in Sicily, one of the greatest mathematicians of all time. He found the law of floating bodies and invented machines to defend his city. He was killed at 75 by a Roman soldier when the Romans took Syracuse.", "Legend says he found his law in the bath and ran down the street shouting “Eureka!”, “I have found it!”.", "His last words to the soldier were said to be: “Do not disturb my circles!”"],
  "Цин Шъхуан": ["He became king at 13 and at 38 united China as its first emperor. He brought in one script, one money and one set of measures for the whole land and began the Great Wall. He died at 49, probably poisoned by mercury pills meant to make him immortal.", "He was buried with the Terracotta Army, about 8,000 clay soldiers, each with a different face.", "The army was found by chance in 1974 by farmers digging a well."],
  "Ханибал": ["A general of Carthage who as a boy swore eternal hatred of Rome. He crossed the Alps with an army and war elephants and crushed the Romans at Cannae. In the end he was defeated, and at about 64 he took poison rather than fall into Roman hands.", "For a long time Romans frightened their children with “Hannibal is at the gates!”.", "Of the roughly 37 elephants he set out with over the Alps, very few survived."],
  "Спартак": ["A Thracian, probably from the lands of today's Bulgaria, captured and sold in Rome as a gladiator. In 73 BC he escaped from the gladiator school and led the greatest slave revolt against Rome, with tens of thousands of followers. He fell in battle at about 40.", "His statue stands in Sandanski, Bulgaria, where tradition says he was born.", "His slaves beat several Roman armies before Rome sent Marcus Crassus against them."],
  "Цицерон": ["The greatest speaker of Rome, a consul and defender of the Republic. After Caesar's murder he attacked Mark Antony in fiery speeches. At 63 he was killed on Antony's orders.", "Hundreds of his letters survive; from them we know how Romans lived day to day.", "He coined the Latin words “qualitas” and “moralis”, which live on in many languages as “quality” and “moral”."],
  "Юлий Цезар": ["A Roman general who conquered Gaul and crossed the river Rubicon with his army to seize power in Rome. He became dictator for life and brought in the calendar with a leap year. On the Ides of March, 44 BC, he was stabbed 23 times by senators, aged 55.", "The month of July is named after him.", "The words “Kaiser” and “tsar” come from his name."],
  "Вергилий": ["A poet born near Mantua in northern Italy. He wrote the “Aeneid”, the poem of the Trojan Aeneas, from whose line the Romans descend. He died at 50 of a fever and asked for the poem to be burned as unfinished, but Emperor Augustus refused.", "Dante chose him as his guide through Hell in the “Divine Comedy”.", "He worked on the “Aeneid” for over 10 years and still thought it unfinished."],
  "Клеопатра": ["The last ruler of ancient Egypt, from the Greek family of the Ptolemies. She allied first with Julius Caesar, then with Mark Antony against Rome. After their defeat she took her own life at 39, by legend with the bite of an asp.", "She was the first of her family to learn Egyptian, and spoke about nine languages.", "She lived closer to our time than to the building of the Great Pyramid."],
  "Октавиан Август": ["Great-nephew and adopted son of Julius Caesar. He defeated Antony and Cleopatra and became the first Roman emperor. He ruled for 41 years of peace, called the “Pax Romana”, and died at 75.", "The month of August is named after him.", "He said: “I found Rome a city of bricks and left it a city of marble.”"],
  "Исус Христос": ["Born in Bethlehem, he grew up in Nazareth. At about 30 he began to preach love of your neighbour and forgiveness, and gathered disciples. At about 33 he was crucified in Jerusalem, and Christians believe he rose on the third day.", "Our calendar counts from his birth, but the monk who worked it out was off by a few years: he was probably born around 4 BC.", "He spoke Aramaic."],
  "Апостол Павел": ["Saul of Tarsus at first hunted Christians, but on the road to Damascus he had a vision and became their most eager preacher. He travelled thousands of kilometres through Asia Minor and Greece founding churches. He was beheaded in Rome in the time of Nero.", "His letters make up almost half the books of the New Testament.", "By trade he was a tentmaker."],
  "Нерон": ["He became Roman emperor at 16. He loved singing and racing, and after the great fire of Rome in 64 he blamed the Christians. When the Senate declared him an enemy, he took his own life at 30.", "His last words were: “What an artist dies in me!”", "The story that he played music while Rome burned is a myth: he was 50 km away, in Antium."],
  "Птолемей": ["An astronomer and geographer from Alexandria. He described the world with the Earth in the middle and the Sun and planets around it, an idea people believed for about 1,400 years. He died at about 70.", "48 of the constellations he described are still used today.", "His maps made the Earth smaller than it is, which is why Columbus thought India was close."],
  "Марк Аврелий": ["A Roman emperor and Stoic philosopher. He spent much of his reign at war along the Danube, and there he wrote his “Meditations” for himself. He died at 58 on campaign, probably of plague.", "His bronze statue on horseback survived because for a long time people thought it showed Constantine the Great.", "He wrote the “Meditations” in Greek, not Latin."],
  "Константин Велики": ["Born in Naissus, today's Niš. The first Roman emperor to embrace Christianity: with the Edict of Milan in 313 he gave Christians freedom. He founded Constantinople and died at 65, baptised on his deathbed.", "He loved Serdica, today's Sofia, and is said to have called it “my Rome”.", "Before a decisive battle he saw a cross in the sky with the words “In this sign you will conquer”."],
  "Свети Августин": ["Born in Thagaste, in today's Algeria. He had a wild youth, but at 32 he became a Christian and later bishop of Hippo. He died at 75 while the Vandals besieged the city.", "His “Confessions” may be the first autobiography in history.", "As a young man he prayed: “Lord, give me chastity, but not yet.”"],
  "Хипатия": ["A mathematician, astronomer and philosopher from Alexandria, daughter of the mathematician Theon. She taught openly to men and women and was respected throughout the city. At about 55 she was killed by an angry mob.", "She is the first woman mathematician whose life we know well enough.", "The 2009 film “Agora” tells her story."],
  "Атила": ["King of the Huns, called “the Scourge of God”. He laid waste to the Balkans, Serdica among them, and then Gaul and Italy. He died at 47 on his wedding night from a heavy nosebleed.", "Pope Leo I persuaded him not to attack Rome.", "He was buried in three coffins, of gold, silver and iron, and his grave has never been found."],
  "Юстиниан I": ["A Byzantine emperor born in a village near today's Skopje. He built the church of Hagia Sophia in Constantinople and gathered the Roman laws into one code. He ruled for 38 years and died at 83.", "His laws are still the basis of law in half of Europe.", "His wife Theodora, a former actress, was his closest adviser."],
  "Мохамед": ["Born in Mecca, he was orphaned young and became a merchant. At 40, Islam teaches, he received the first revelation in a cave near Mecca. He founded Islam and died at 62 in Medina.", "The Islamic calendar starts with his move from Mecca to Medina in 622.", "Tradition says he could not read or write."],
  "Хан Аспарух": ["Son of Khan Kubrat, who led his Bulgars from the steppes to the Danube. In 680 he crushed the Byzantine army at Ongal, and in 681 Byzantium recognised the new state, Danube Bulgaria. He fell around 700 in battle with the Khazars.", "Bulgaria is one of the few European states that has kept its name for more than 1,300 years.", "Some scholars think his grave is near the village of Voznesenka in Ukraine."],
  "Карл Велики": ["King of the Franks who united much of Western Europe. On Christmas Day 800 the pope crowned him emperor in Rome. He supported schools and books and died at 71 in Aachen.", "The Slavic word for king, “kral”, comes from his name.", "He himself could barely write and kept tablets under his pillow to practise."],
  "Хан Крум": ["A Bulgarian khan called “the Fearsome”. He took Serdica in 809, and in 811 he crushed the Byzantine emperor Nikephoros in the Varbitsa pass. He died suddenly at 59 while preparing to besiege Constantinople.", "He made the skull of Nikephoros into a cup lined with silver.", "He is the first Bulgarian ruler with written laws: theft, lying and drunkenness were punished."],
  "Ал-Хорезми": ["A Persian scholar at the House of Wisdom in Baghdad. He wrote a book on solving equations and spread the Indian numerals with zero. He died at about 70.", "The word “algebra” comes from the title of his book (“al-jabr”).", "The word “algorithm” comes from his name."],
  "Свети Методий": ["The elder brother of Constantine-Cyril, born in Thessaloniki. Before becoming a monk he governed a Slavic province of Byzantium. The two went to Great Moravia, and after Cyril's death he became archbishop and died there at about 70 in 885.", "German bishops kept him in prison for two and a half years for holding services in Slavic.", "Tradition says he translated almost the whole Bible in six months."],
  "Свети Кирил": ["Born in Thessaloniki as Constantine, called “the Philosopher” for his learning. Around 855–863 he created Glagolitic, the first Slavic alphabet, and with his brother Methodius translated the church books. He died at 42 in Rome as a monk named Cyril.", "The Cyrillic alphabet bears his name, but it was made later by his students in Bulgaria.", "He spoke many languages and debated faith with the Arabs and the Khazars."],
  "Цар Борис I": ["He baptised Bulgaria in 864 and took the name Michael. He welcomed the students of Cyril and Methodius, Clement and Naum, and with them Bulgaria became the centre of Slavic writing. He withdrew to a monastery and died there at 72; he is honoured as a saint.", "He left the monastery to depose his son Vladimir, who wanted to bring back the old gods, and put Simeon on the throne.", "In 893 the Slavic language became official in the church and the state."],
  "Алфред Велики": ["King of Wessex in England who stopped the Viking invasion. He built fortified towns and had important books translated into English. He died at 50 and is the only English king called “the Great”.", "Legend says that while hiding from the Vikings in a hut he let the housewife's cakes burn, and she scolded him without knowing who he was.", "He built the first English fleet."],
  "Цар Симеон Велики": ["Son of Boris I, educated in Constantinople, which is why he was called “half-Greek”. Under him Preslav saw the Golden Age of Bulgarian letters. In 917 he crushed the Byzantines at Achelous, took the title of tsar and died at 63.", "Under him Bulgaria reached three seas: the Black, the Aegean and the Adriatic.", "A legend says the Byzantines cut the head off a statue they believed was bound to his life, and he died that same day."],
  "Свети Иван Рилски": ["A hermit who lived in a cave and in a hollow tree in the Rila mountains. Disciples gathered around him, and so the Rila Monastery began. He died at about 70 and is the patron saint of the Bulgarian people.", "Tsar Peter came to meet him, but the saint refused; the two only bowed to each other from afar.", "His relics are still in the Rila Monastery."],
  "Авицена": ["A Persian physician and philosopher who by 18 knew all the sciences of his day. His “Canon of Medicine” was studied in European universities for about 600 years. He wrote about 450 works and died at 57 of illness.", "At 17 he cured the emir and was given the run of the royal library.", "He was among the first to describe that some diseases spread through water and soil."],
  "Омар Хайям": ["A Persian mathematician, astronomer and poet. He made a calendar more accurate than the one we use today. He is famous for his quatrains, the rubaiyat, about wine, love and the shortness of life. He died at 83 in Nishapur.", "He solved cubic equations using geometry.", "A crater on the Moon and an asteroid are named after him."],
  "Хилдегард фон Бинген": ["A German abbess who had visions from childhood. She wrote about healing plants, medicine and nature and composed music. She founded a convent near Bingen on the Rhine and died at 81.", "She is one of the first women composers whose music we know by name.", "She invented her own secret language of about 1,000 words."],
  "Саладин": ["A Kurdish sultan of Egypt and Syria. In 1187 he took Jerusalem from the crusaders but spared its people. Even his enemies respected him, and he died at 55 in Damascus of a fever.", "He gave so much away that when he died there was not even money for his funeral.", "When Richard the Lionheart lost his horse in battle, Saladin sent him two."],
  "Ричард Лъвското сърце": ["King of England who led the Third Crusade. On his way home he was captured in Austria and freed for a huge ransom. He died at 41 from a crossbow bolt during a siege in France.", "Of his ten years as king he spent only about six months in England and hardly spoke English.", "He was held prisoner in Dürnstein castle on the Danube."],
  "Чингис хан": ["Born Temüjin, he grew up poor after his father was murdered. He united the Mongol tribes and in 1206 became Great Khan. He founded what soon became the largest land empire in history and died at 65; his grave is unknown.", "He set up a postal service with riders and stations across the whole empire.", "Millions of men alive today are thought to descend from him."],
  "Франциск от Асизи": ["Son of a rich cloth merchant from Assisi, he gave up everything to live in poverty. He founded the Franciscan order and preached love for every creature. He died at 44.", "He preached to the birds, and legend says he tamed the wolf of Gubbio.", "In 1223 he made the first living nativity scene, with a real ox and donkey."],
  "Руми": ["A Persian poet born in Balkh, in today's Afghanistan. His family fled the Mongols and settled in Konya, Turkey. His friendship with the dervish Shams of Tabriz made him a poet of divine love. He died at 66.", "His followers founded the order of the whirling dervishes.", "Today he is one of the best-selling poets in the United States."],
  "Тома Аквински": ["An Italian Dominican friar who joined Aristotle's teaching with Christianity. He wrote the huge “Summa Theologica”. He died at 49 on his way to a council in Lyon.", "His family locked him in a castle for a year to stop him becoming a friar; it did not work.", "His fellow students called him “the Dumb Ox” because he was big and quiet."],
  "Марко Поло": ["A Venetian who at 17 set off with his father and uncle for China. He served Kublai Khan for 17 years and travelled all over Asia. He told his travels in a Genoese prison and died at 69 in Venice.", "On his deathbed he said: “I have not told half of what I saw.”", "His tales of paper money and black stones that burn (coal) amazed Europe."],
  "Данте Алигиери": ["A poet from Florence, author of the “Divine Comedy”, a journey through Hell, Purgatory and Heaven. He wrote it not in Latin but in the spoken Tuscan tongue, and so shaped the Italian language. Banished from Florence for ever, he died at 56 in Ravenna of malaria.", "If he ever returned to Florence, he was sentenced to be burned.", "Beatrice, the girl he loved from the age of nine, guides him through Heaven."],
  "Йоханес Гутенберг": ["A goldsmith from Mainz who around 1450 invented the printing press with movable metal type. He printed about 180 Bibles, of which 49 survive. He went bankrupt, lost his workshop to his partner and died at 68.", "Books became affordable, and knowledge spread faster than ever before.", "In his old age the archbishop gave him a pension: clothes, grain and wine every year."],
  "Жана д’Арк": ["A peasant girl from Domrémy who heard the voices of saints. At 17 she led the French army, lifted the siege of Orléans and brought Charles VII to his crowning. She was captured and at 19 burned at the stake in Rouen.", "She could not read and signed with a cross.", "25 years after her death she was declared innocent, and in 1920 a saint."],
  "Христофор Колумб": ["A sailor from Genoa who wanted to reach Asia by sailing west. In 1492, with three ships, he reached the Bahamas. He made four voyages and died at 54, sure he had been to Asia.", "America is named not after him but after Amerigo Vespucci.", "His remains were moved so many times that both Seville and Santo Domingo claim to have them."],
  "Леонардо да Винчи": ["Born in Vinci near Florence, the painter of the “Mona Lisa” and “The Last Supper”. In his notebooks he drew flying machines, tanks and the inside of the human body. He died at 67 in France, at the court of King Francis I.", "He was left-handed and wrote in mirror script, right to left.", "He kept the “Mona Lisa” with him until the end of his life."],
  "Албрехт Дюрер": ["A painter and engraver from Nuremberg, the greatest German master of his time. He is famous for his self-portraits, the “Praying Hands” and the “Young Hare”. He died at 56 in his home town.", "He was the first artist to sign his work with a mark, “AD”, like a trademark.", "He made a famous rhinoceros print without ever seeing one, only from a description in a letter."],
  "Николай Коперник": ["Born in Toruń, Poland. He showed that the Earth and the planets go round the Sun, not the other way round. His book came out in the year of his death, at 70; legend says he saw the first copy on his deathbed.", "Besides being an astronomer he was a doctor and wrote about money.", "His grave was found in 2005 and identified by DNA from a hair found in one of his books."],
  "Микеланджело": ["A sculptor, painter and architect born in Caprese, Tuscany. At 26 he began “David”, and later he painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel. He designed the dome of St Peter's and died at 88 in Rome.", "He painted the ceiling standing on a scaffold for about four years, head bent back.", "After his death his body was smuggled out of Rome to be buried in Florence."],
  "Мартин Лутер": ["A monk and professor in Wittenberg, born in Eisleben. In 1517 he set out 95 theses against the selling of indulgences, and so the Reformation began. He translated the Bible into German and died at 62 in the town where he was born.", "He became a monk after a vow made in a terrible storm, when lightning struck next to him.", "His translation shaped the modern German language."],
  "Рафаел": ["A painter from Urbino, one of the three great masters of the Renaissance. He painted “The School of Athens” in the Vatican and the “Sistine Madonna”. He died at 37, on his very birthday.", "The two little angels at the bottom of the “Sistine Madonna” are among the most copied pictures in the world.", "He is buried in the Pantheon in Rome."],
  "Хенри VIII": ["King of England, famous for his six wives: he divorced two and had two beheaded. He broke with the pope and founded the Church of England. He died at 55, ill and very fat.", "His suits of armour show how his waist grew over the years.", "The pope gave him the title “Defender of the Faith” for a book against Luther, and its abbreviation is still on British coins."],
  "Сюлейман Великолепни": ["An Ottoman sultan who ruled for 46 years, when the empire was at its height. He besieged Vienna in 1529 and made so many laws that the Turks call him Kanuni, the Lawgiver. He died at 71 during a siege in Hungary.", "His death was kept secret for weeks so the army would not fall apart.", "He married Hürrem, a former slave who became one of the most powerful women in the empire."],
  "Елизабет I": ["Daughter of Henry VIII and Anne Boleyn, whom her father had beheaded. She never married and said she was married to England. Under her England beat the Spanish Armada and Shakespeare's theatre flourished. She died at 69.", "The US state of Virginia is named after her, “the Virgin Queen”.", "She spoke several languages and translated books from Latin and Greek."],
  "Мигел де Сервантес": ["A Spanish writer who as a soldier lost the use of his left hand at the battle of Lepanto. Pirates captured him and he spent five years as a slave in Algiers. In his later years he wrote “Don Quixote”, the first modern novel, and died at 68 in Madrid.", "He died at almost the same time as Shakespeare, which is why 23 April is World Book Day.", "He tried four times to escape from slavery."],
  "Уилям Шекспир": ["Born in Stratford-upon-Avon, an actor and playwright in London. He wrote about 39 plays, among them “Hamlet” and “Romeo and Juliet”, and 154 sonnets. He died at 52, probably on his birthday.", "He coined more than 1,700 words that are still part of English.", "In his will he left his wife his “second-best bed”."],
  "Галилео Галилей": ["A scientist from Pisa who improved the telescope and discovered the moons of Jupiter and the mountains of the Moon. He backed Copernicus, and in 1633 the Inquisition tried him and kept him under house arrest for the rest of his life. He went blind and died at 77.", "Legend says that after the verdict he whispered: “And yet it moves!”", "The Church officially admitted its mistake only in 1992."],
  "Йоханес Кеплер": ["A German astronomer born in Weil der Stadt. As Tycho Brahe's assistant he found the three laws by which planets move in ellipses around the Sun. He died at 58 in Regensburg.", "For years he defended his mother, accused of witchcraft, and got her freed.", "He wrote one of the first science fiction stories, about a journey to the Moon."],
  "Рене Декарт": ["A French philosopher and mathematician who lived for a long time in the Netherlands. His is the thought “I think, therefore I am”, and the coordinate system bears his name: Cartesian. He went to Stockholm to teach Queen Christina and died there at 53 of pneumonia.", "He liked to stay in bed until late, but the queen made him teach at 5 in the morning.", "Legend says the idea of coordinates came to him while watching a fly on the ceiling."],
  "Рембранд": ["A Dutch painter from Leiden, a master of light and shadow. He painted “The Night Watch” and dozens of self-portraits all through his life. He went bankrupt and died poor at 63 in Amsterdam.", "“The Night Watch” is really a daytime scene; old varnish made it dark.", "His self-portraits show him growing older year by year."],
  "Блез Паскал": ["A French mathematician and philosopher. At 19 he built a calculating machine to help his father, a tax collector. With Fermat he founded probability theory, and he died at 39 after a long illness.", "The unit of pressure, the pascal, and a programming language are named after him.", "He started one of the first public transport lines in Paris: carriages on a fixed timetable."],
  "Антони ван Левенхук": ["A cloth merchant from Delft who ground his own lenses and made hundreds of microscopes. He was the first to see bacteria, red blood cells and other “little animals” in water. He died at 90.", "He never went to university and knew no Latin; he wrote his findings in Dutch.", "He kept secret how he made his lenses."],
  "Луи XIV": ["A French king called the Sun King. He became king at 4 and ruled for 72 years, longer than any other monarch in European history. He built the palace of Versailles and died at 76 of gangrene.", "The name “Sun” comes from a ballet in which, as a young man, he danced the part of the Sun.", "He outlived his son and grandson, so his great-grandson succeeded him."],
  "Исак Нютон": ["An English scientist who found the law of universal gravitation and the three laws of motion. He split light into colours with a prism and invented calculus. He died at 84 and was buried in Westminster Abbey.", "The apple story is at least half true: he told it himself in old age, but the apple did not hit his head.", "For many years he ran England's Royal Mint and personally hunted down forgers."],
  "Петър Велики": ["A Russian tsar over two metres tall who wanted to make Russia European. He travelled through Europe in disguise and worked as a carpenter in Dutch shipyards. He founded Saint Petersburg and died at 52.", "He brought in a tax on beards: whoever wanted to keep one had to pay.", "He learned more than ten trades, including pulling teeth."],
  "Антонио Вивалди": ["A Venetian composer and priest, called “the Red Priest” for his red hair. He taught music at an orphanage for girls and wrote “The Four Seasons” and about 500 concertos. He died poor in Vienna at 63.", "His music was forgotten for about 200 years and rediscovered only in the 20th century.", "The orphanage girls became a famous orchestra that people came from all over Europe to hear."],
  "Йохан Себастиан Бах": ["A German composer and organist from Eisenach, cantor of St Thomas's church in Leipzig. He wrote more than 1,000 works and had 20 children. He died at 65 after a failed eye operation.", "As a young man he walked about 400 km to hear the famous organist Buxtehude.", "His music flies through space on the golden record of the Voyager probes."],
  "Георг Фридрих Хендел": ["A composer born in Halle, Germany, who spent most of his life in London. He wrote the oratorio “Messiah” and the “Water Music”, played on a barge on the Thames for the king. He went blind and died at 74; he is buried in Westminster Abbey.", "Audiences stand for the “Hallelujah” chorus, by tradition because the king once did.", "He became a British subject, and the English count him as their own composer."],
  "Волтер": ["A French writer and philosopher, real name François-Marie Arouet. He mocked injustice sharply and fought for tolerance and free speech, for which he was thrown into the Bastille and exiled. He wrote “Candide” and died at 83 in Paris.", "He got rich when he and a mathematician found a flaw in a state lottery and bought up all the tickets.", "Stories say he drank dozens of cups of coffee a day."],
  "Бенджамин Франклин": ["Born in Boston as the 15th of 17 children, a printer, scientist and statesman. With a kite he showed that lightning is electricity, and he invented the lightning rod and bifocal glasses. One of the founders of the United States, he signed the Declaration of Independence and died at 84 in Philadelphia.", "His face is on the 100-dollar bill.", "He never patented his inventions; he wanted them to help everyone."],
  "Карл Линей": ["A Swedish scientist who ordered plants and animals and gave every kind two Latin names, such as Homo sapiens. He described thousands of species and taught students who travelled all over the world. He died at 70 in Uppsala.", "He came up with a “flower clock”: flowers that open at different hours of the day.", "He was made a nobleman and became Carl von Linné."],
  "Фридрих Велики": ["King of Prussia who played the flute, wrote music and was a friend of Voltaire. He fought the Seven Years' War and made Prussia a great power. He died at 74 at the palace of Sanssouci near Potsdam.", "He got farmers to plant potatoes with a trick: he had his own fields guarded by soldiers so they would seem valuable.", "He wished to be buried beside his dogs; it was done only in 1991. Today people leave potatoes on his grave."],
  "Мария Терезия": ["The only woman to rule the Habsburg lands. She brought in compulsory schooling in 1774 and reformed the state. She had 16 children, among them the French queen Marie Antoinette, and died at 63 in Vienna.", "The thaler with her portrait kept being minted after her death and was used as money in Africa and Arabia into the 20th century.", "She wrote thousands of letters of motherly advice to her children."],
  "Паисий Хилендарски": ["A monk of the Hilandar monastery on Mount Athos, born in Bansko. In 1762 he finished his “Slavonic-Bulgarian History” to show the Bulgarians they had a glorious past and urge them not to be ashamed of their language. He died at 51 while travelling through the Bulgarian lands.", "“O foolish one, why are you ashamed to call yourself a Bulgarian?” is the most famous line of his book.", "The first copy of the book was made by Sophronius of Vratsa in 1765; from then on it passed from hand to hand."],
  "Имануел Кант": ["A German philosopher from Königsberg who hardly ever left his home town. He wrote the “Critique of Pure Reason” and taught that you should act as you would want everyone to act. He died at 79.", "He was so punctual that his neighbours set their clocks by his daily walk.", "He missed his walk only once: when he was reading Rousseau's “Emile”."],
  "Екатерина Велика": ["A German princess born in Stettin who married the future Russian tsar Peter III. She overthrew him and ruled Russia for 34 years, extending the empire to the Black Sea. She died at 67.", "She had herself inoculated against smallpox to show her people it was safe.", "Her collection of paintings was the beginning of the Hermitage museum."],
  "Джордж Вашингтон": ["A landowner from Virginia who led the army in the American War of Independence. He became the first president of the United States and refused a third term. He died at 67 of a throat infection.", "His false teeth were not wooden but made of ivory, metal and human teeth.", "The capital of the United States bears his name."],
  "Джеймс Уат": ["A Scottish instrument maker who improved the steam engine by adding a separate condenser. His engine made the Industrial Revolution possible. He died at 83.", "The unit of power, the watt, is named after him.", "He came up with “horsepower” to explain to people how strong his engine was."],
  "Жозеф-Мишел Монголфие": ["A French paper maker who with his brother Étienne invented the hot-air balloon. In 1783 their balloon lifted the first living creatures into the sky, and soon the first people. He died at 69.", "The first passengers were a sheep, a duck and a rooster, and all three landed alive.", "The idea came to him watching laundry billow upwards over a fire."],
  "Едуард Дженър": ["An English country doctor who noticed that milkmaids who had had cowpox did not catch smallpox. In 1796 he vaccinated a boy and so made the first vaccine. He died at 73.", "The word “vaccine” comes from the Latin vacca, cow.", "By 1980 smallpox had vanished from the world thanks to his vaccine."],
  "Йохан Волфганг фон Гьоте": ["A German poet from Frankfurt, author of “Faust” and “The Sorrows of Young Werther”. For many years he was a minister in Weimar, and he also did science, writing about colours and plants. He died at 82 in Weimar.", "His last words were: “More light!”", "After “Werther”, young men across Europe dressed like its hero, in a blue coat and yellow waistcoat."],
  "Волфганг Амадеус Моцарт": ["The child wonder from Salzburg who composed from the age of 5 and toured Europe with his father. He wrote more than 600 works, among them “The Magic Flute” and the unfinished “Requiem”. He died at 35 in Vienna.", "At 14 he wrote down from memory a whole forbidden church piece after hearing it just once.", "He loved billiards and had a pet starling that could whistle the start of one of his concertos."],
  "Фридрих Шилер": ["A German poet and playwright from Marbach, author of “The Robbers” and “William Tell”. He was a close friend of Goethe in Weimar. He died at 45 of tuberculosis.", "His “Ode to Joy” is in Beethoven's Ninth Symphony, and its tune is now the anthem of the European Union.", "As a young man he fled from the duke who had forbidden him to write plays."],
  "Робърт Фултън": ["An American who started out as a painter. In 1807 he launched the first successful steamboat, the “Clermont”, on the Hudson River. He died at 49 after a chill caught in a hard winter.", "Before the steamboat he built a submarine for Napoleon, called the “Nautilus”.", "People called his steamboat “Fulton's Folly”, until it moved."],
  "Наполеон Бонапарт": ["Born on the island of Corsica, he became a general and then emperor of the French. He conquered much of Europe and left the Napoleonic Code, laws still used today. After his defeat at Waterloo he was exiled to the island of Saint Helena and died there at 51.", "He was not short: about 1.69 m, average for his time. The myth comes from British cartoons.", "His march on Russia in 1812 began with about 600,000 soldiers; few came back."],
  "Александър фон Хумболт": ["A German scientist and explorer from Berlin. He spent five years exploring South America and climbed the volcano Chimborazo higher than anyone before him. He wrote “Cosmos”, a description of all of nature, and died at 89.", "More plants, animals and places are named after him than after any other person.", "Charles Darwin set out on his voyage inspired by his books."],
  "Лудвиг ван Бетовен": ["A composer from Bonn who lived in Vienna. He began to go deaf at about 30, yet wrote his greatest works, including the Ninth Symphony, when he could barely hear. He died at 56, and about 20,000 people came to his funeral.", "He counted out 60 coffee beans for every cup.", "The “Ode to Joy” tune from his Ninth Symphony is the anthem of the European Union."],
  "Джейн Остин": ["An English writer, daughter of a country clergyman. She wrote “Pride and Prejudice” and “Sense and Sensibility”, witty novels about love and marriage. She died at 41 in Winchester.", "Her books came out without her name, only “By a Lady”.", "Her face is on the British 10-pound note."],
  "Карл Фридрих Гаус": ["A German mathematician from Brunswick, called “the prince of mathematicians”. He also worked in astronomy and magnetism and with Weber built one of the first electric telegraphs. He died at 77 in Göttingen.", "As a schoolboy he added up the numbers from 1 to 100 in seconds: 50 pairs of 101.", "His bell curve and his face were on the old German 10-mark note."],
  "Джордж Стивънсън": ["An English miner's son who learned to read only at 18. He built the first real railways and, with his son Robert, the locomotive “Rocket”. He is called “the father of railways” and died at 67.", "The track width he chose, 1,435 mm, is still the standard in most of the world.", "He studied at night after working all day in the mines."],
  "Якоб Грим": ["A German scholar of language from Hanau who with his brother Wilhelm collected folk tales. Among them are “Snow White”, “Cinderella” and “Hansel and Gretel”. He began the huge German Dictionary and died at 78 in Berlin.", "The dictionary the brothers began was finished only in 1961.", "Both were dismissed from the university of Göttingen for protesting against the king."],
  "Луи Дагер": ["A French painter of stage scenery who invented the daguerreotype, the first practical way of taking photographs. In 1839 France gave the invention to the whole world. He died at 63.", "One of his first photos of a Paris street shows a single person: he stood still while his shoes were being shined.", "Before photography he made “dioramas”, huge pictures that changed with the light."],
  "Лорд Байрон": ["An English poet, author of “Don Juan” and “Childe Harold”, famous for his scandals too. He left England and went to fight for the freedom of Greece. He died at 36 of a fever in Missolonghi and is a national hero in Greece.", "Dogs were banned at Cambridge, so he kept a bear.", "He was the father of Ada Lovelace, the first programmer."],
  "Самюъл Морз": ["An American painter who became an inventor. He made the electric telegraph and the code of dots and dashes named after him. His first message, in 1844, was “What hath God wrought”. He died at 80.", "The idea came to him after his wife died while he was away and the news reached him too late.", "“SOS” in Morse code is three dots, three dashes, three dots."],
  "Майкъл Фарадей": ["A blacksmith's son from London with almost no schooling: he was apprenticed to a bookbinder and read the books he bound. He discovered electromagnetic induction and made the first electric motor and dynamo. He died at 75.", "He began Christmas lectures for children that are still held today.", "He turned down a knighthood; he wanted to remain “plain Mr Faraday”."],
  "Франц Шуберт": ["A Viennese composer who wrote over 600 songs, among them “The Erl-King”, composed at 17. He lived poor, and his friends held evenings of his music, “Schubertiads”. He died at 31 and was buried near Beethoven, as he wished.", "On some days he wrote eight songs.", "His “Unfinished” Symphony has only two movements."],
  "Александър Пушкин": ["A Russian poet from Moscow, founder of modern Russian literature and author of “Eugene Onegin”. For his free-spirited verses he was exiled to the south. He died at 37 after a duel fought for his wife's honour.", "His great-grandfather was African: Abram Gannibal, godson of Peter the Great.", "He also wrote fairy tales in verse, such as “The Tale of the Fisherman and the Fish”."],
  "Виктор Юго": ["A French writer from Besançon, author of “Les Misérables” and “The Hunchback of Notre-Dame”. He spent 19 years in exile on the island of Guernsey for opposing Napoleon III. He died at 83, and about two million people turned out for his funeral.", "After “Les Misérables” came out he wrote to his publisher just “?”, and the reply was just “!”.", "His novel about Notre-Dame helped save the cathedral from being torn down."],
  "Юстус фон Либих": ["A German chemist from Darmstadt who found that plants need minerals and so created artificial fertiliser. He brought in teaching in the laboratory, and chemists from all over the world learned that way. He died at 69 in Munich.", "As a pharmacist's apprentice he was sent away after causing an explosion.", "He invented “Liebig's Extract of Meat”, and its jars came with famous collectable picture cards."],
  "Чарлз Дарвин": ["An English naturalist who sailed round the world for five years on HMS “Beagle”. In 1859 he published “On the Origin of Species” and explained how living things change through natural selection. He died at 73 and was buried in Westminster Abbey.", "The tortoises and finches of the Galápagos Islands gave him the idea.", "He studied earthworms for decades and wrote a whole book about them."],
  "Ейбрахам Линкълн": ["Born in a log cabin in Kentucky, he taught himself to be a lawyer. As the 16th president of the United States he led the country through the Civil War and abolished slavery. At 56 he was shot at Ford's Theatre in Washington.", "At 1.93 m he was the tallest US president.", "He is the only president with a patent, for a device to lift boats over shallows."],
  "Фредерик Шопен": ["A Polish composer and pianist born near Warsaw, called “the poet of the piano”. At 20 he left Poland and lived in Paris, where he was with the writer George Sand. He died at 39 of tuberculosis.", "His heart was carried to Warsaw in a jar of cognac and sealed in the Holy Cross Church.", "He gave his first concert at 7."],
  "Чарлз Дикенс": ["An English writer who as a child worked in a shoe-polish factory while his father was in a debtors' prison. He wrote “Oliver Twist”, “David Copperfield” and “A Christmas Carol”. He died at 58.", "“A Christmas Carol” shaped the way we still celebrate Christmas.", "His novels came out in parts, and in America people waited at the docks for the ship with the next one."],
  "Рихард Вагнер": ["A German composer from Leipzig, author of the huge opera cycle “The Ring of the Nibelung”, about 15 hours of music. With the help of King Ludwig II of Bavaria he built his own theatre in Bayreuth. He died at 69 in Venice.", "The wedding march from his opera “Lohengrin” is played at weddings all over the world.", "He fled his creditors by ship through a terrible storm, which inspired “The Flying Dutchman”."],
  "Ада Лъвлейс": ["An English mathematician, daughter of the poet Byron. She wrote notes on Charles Babbage's “Analytical Engine”, and in them the first computer program. She died at 36 and was buried beside the father she never knew.", "She foresaw that one day machines would be able to write music.", "The programming language Ada is named after her."],
  "Ото фон Бисмарк": ["A Prussian statesman called “the Iron Chancellor”. In 1871 he united Germany and became its first chancellor. He brought in health insurance and pensions, the first in the world, and died at 83.", "As a student he fought about 25 duels.", "The pickled “Bismarck herring” is named after him."],
  "Карл Маркс": ["A German philosopher from Trier. With Friedrich Engels he wrote the “Communist Manifesto”, and later “Capital”. He lived poor in London for many years and died there at 64.", "Only about 11 people came to his funeral.", "The last line of the manifesto is “Workers of the world, unite!”."],
  "Кралица Виктория": ["Queen of Great Britain for 63 years; a whole age bears her name. Her nine children married into the royal families of Europe, and she was called “the grandmother of Europe”. She died at 81.", "She made the white wedding dress fashionable.", "After her husband Albert died she wore black for 40 years."],
  "Джоузеф Базалгет": ["An English engineer who after London's “Great Stink” of 1858 built the city's sewers. Over 1,700 km of pipes carried away the dirty water and ended the cholera epidemics. He died at 71.", "He made the pipes twice as wide as needed, “we're only going to do this once”, which is why his system still works today.", "He also built the embankments along the Thames."],
  "Флорънс Найтингейл": ["An English nurse named after Florence, the city where she was born. In the Crimean War her care saved thousands of wounded soldiers, and she made her rounds at night with a lamp: “the Lady with the Lamp”. She founded the first school for nurses and died at 90.", "International Nurses Day is on her birthday, 12 May.", "She was a statistician too and invented a new kind of chart to show what the soldiers were dying of."],
  "Георги Раковски": ["A revolutionary and writer from Kotel who was the first to plan an organised struggle for Bulgaria's liberation. In 1862 he formed the First Bulgarian Legion in Belgrade. He died at 46 of tuberculosis in Bucharest.", "He changed his name many times; he was born Sabi Stoykov Popovich.", "He wrote the poem “The Forest Traveller”, a call to the Bulgarians to rise up."],
  "Фьодор Достоевски": ["A Russian writer from Moscow, author of “Crime and Punishment”, “The Idiot” and “The Brothers Karamazov”. In 1849 he was sentenced to death but pardoned at the last moment before the firing squad, and spent four years in a prison camp in Siberia. He died at 59.", "He wrote “The Gambler” in 26 days to pay off his debts and married the stenographer to whom he dictated it.", "All his life he struggled with a passion for gambling."],
  "Луи Пастьор": ["A French scientist who proved that many diseases are caused by germs. He invented pasteurisation, gentle heating that keeps milk and wine from spoiling. In 1885 he saved a boy bitten by a rabid dog with the first rabies vaccine. He died at 72.", "Three of his five children died of typhoid, which drove him to search for ways to beat disease.", "The boy he saved, Joseph Meister, later became caretaker at the Pasteur Institute."],
  "Грегор Мендел": ["A monk in a monastery in Brno, born in Silesia. In the monastery garden he grew about 28,000 pea plants and discovered the laws of heredity. Nobody understood his discovery in his lifetime; he died at 61 as abbot.", "He failed the teacher's exam twice.", "His work was rediscovered only in 1900, and he became “the father of genetics”."],
  "Лев Толстой": ["A Russian writer born on the estate of Yasnaya Polyana, author of “War and Peace” and “Anna Karenina”. In old age he preached simple living and non-violence and inspired Gandhi. At 82 he secretly left home and died at a small railway station.", "His wife copied “War and Peace” out by hand about seven times.", "“War and Peace” has about 580 characters."],
  "Дмитрий Менделеев": ["A Russian chemist from Tobolsk in Siberia, the youngest of many children. In 1869 he arranged the chemical elements in a table and left gaps for ones not yet found, and they really were found. He died at 72.", "Legend says he saw the table in a dream.", "Element number 101 is called mendelevium."],
  "Марк Твен": ["An American writer, real name Samuel Clemens, who grew up on the banks of the Mississippi. He worked as a steamboat pilot, then wrote “The Adventures of Tom Sawyer” and “Huckleberry Finn”. He died at 74.", "He was born in a year of Halley's Comet and predicted he would go out with it, and he died when it returned in 1910.", "“Mark twain” was the river pilots' call for a depth of two fathoms."],
  "Васил Левски": ["Born in Karlovo as Vasil Kunchev, a former monk called “the Deacon”. He travelled the land and built a network of secret revolutionary committees to free Bulgaria. He was captured near Lovech and hanged in Sofia at 35.", "He is called “the Apostle of Freedom”.", "He got the nickname “Levski”, “lion-like”, for jumping like a lion."],
  "Пьотр Чайковски": ["A Russian composer, author of the ballets “Swan Lake”, “The Nutcracker” and “The Sleeping Beauty”. For 13 years a rich admirer supported him with money; they only wrote letters and never met. He died at 53 in Saint Petersburg.", "He conducted at the opening of Carnegie Hall in New York in 1891.", "As a little boy he cried on hearing music and said: “This music is in my head and won't leave me alone!”"],
  "Клод Моне": ["A French painter from Paris, the father of Impressionism. The whole movement is named after his painting “Impression, Sunrise”. In his last years he painted the water lilies in his garden at Giverny, and he died at 86.", "He painted about 250 pictures of water lilies.", "In old age cataracts changed the colours he saw, and his paintings grew redder."],
  "Роберт Кох": ["A German doctor who discovered the bacteria that cause anthrax, tuberculosis and cholera. In 1905 he received the Nobel Prize. He died at 66 in Baden-Baden.", "World Tuberculosis Day, 24 March, is the day he announced his discovery.", "He started as a country doctor with a microscope his wife gave him."],
  "Карл Бенц": ["A German engineer from Karlsruhe who in 1886 patented the first real car with a petrol engine. His company later became Mercedes-Benz. He died at 84.", "His father, a locomotive driver, died when Karl was 2.", "His first car had only three wheels."],
  "Фридрих Ницше": ["A German philosopher who became a professor in Basel at only 24. He wrote “Thus Spoke Zarathustra” and the famous “God is dead”. In 1889 in Turin he broke down mentally and spent his last 11 years ill; he died at 55.", "Stories say that when he broke down he embraced a horse being beaten in the street.", "He also composed music."],
  "Вилхелм Рьонтген": ["A German physicist who in 1895 discovered rays that can see through the body: X-rays. In 1901 he received the very first Nobel Prize in Physics. He died at 77.", "The first X-ray picture was of his wife's hand, with her ring.", "He refused to patent his discovery so that it would benefit everyone."],
  "Томас Едисон": ["An American inventor with more than 1,000 patents: the phonograph, the practical electric light bulb, the film camera and the first power stations. He went to school for only three months. He died at 84.", "He was almost deaf from youth.", "He said: “Genius is one percent inspiration and ninety-nine percent perspiration.”"],
  "Христо Ботев": ["A poet and revolutionary from Kalofer. In Romania he published newspapers and wrote poems about freedom. In 1876 his band seized the Austrian steamer “Radetzky”, landed at Kozloduy, and he fell at 28 in the Vratsa mountains.", "Every year on 2 June at noon, sirens in Bulgaria sound for a minute in memory of him and of all who died for freedom.", "A replica of the steamer “Radetzky” now stands in Kozloduy."],
  "Берта Бенц": ["Karl Benz's wife, who put her dowry into his car. In 1888, without telling her husband, she set off with their two sons on the first long car journey: 106 km from Mannheim to Pforzheim. She died at 95.", "On the way she cleared a blocked pipe with a hatpin and insulated a wire with her garter.", "She bought fuel at a pharmacy in Wiesloch, now counted as the world's first filling station."],
  "Иван Вазов": ["A writer from Sopot called “the patriarch of Bulgarian literature”. In exile in Odessa he wrote “Under the Yoke”, the first Bulgarian novel, and the “Epic of the Forgotten”. He was also minister of education. He died at 71 in Sofia.", "“Under the Yoke” has been translated into more than 30 languages.", "He never married."],
  "Винсент ван Гог": ["A Dutch painter who made about 900 paintings in only ten years or so, among them “Sunflowers” and “The Starry Night”. He lived poor and suffered severe mental crises, in one of which he cut off part of his ear. He died at 37 of a gunshot wound.", "He sold only one painting in his lifetime.", "He wrote more than 650 letters to his brother Theo, who supported him all his life."],
  "Никола Тесла": ["Born in Smiljan, in today's Croatia. He invented the alternating current system that still powers our homes. He died poor and alone in a New York hotel room at 86.", "The unit of magnetic flux density, the tesla, is named after him.", "He loved pigeons and looked after them to the end of his life."],
  "Зигмунд Фройд": ["An Austrian doctor born in Moravia who lived in Vienna. He founded psychoanalysis and taught that dreams and the unconscious steer much of our lives. In 1938 he fled the Nazis to London and died there at 83.", "His famous patients' couch is now in his museum in London.", "He smoked about 20 cigars a day."],
  "Макс Планк": ["A German physicist from Kiel who in 1900 started quantum theory. In 1918 he received the Nobel Prize. He died at 89.", "His teacher advised him not to study physics, as “almost everything has already been discovered”.", "He was an excellent pianist and often played music with Einstein."],
  "Алеко Константинов": ["A writer from Svishtov called “the Happy One”. He created Bay Ganyo and wrote the travel book “To Chicago and Back” after visiting the World's Fair. At 34 he was shot by mistake near Pazardzhik; the bullet was meant for someone else.", "In 1895 he led about 300 people up Cherni Vrah and so started organised hiking in Bulgaria.", "Bay Ganyo became so popular that his name is still used for a certain type of person."],
  "Мария Кюри": ["Born in Warsaw, she discovered polonium and radium with her husband Pierre. The first woman to win a Nobel Prize and the only person with Nobel Prizes in two different sciences, physics and chemistry. She died at 66 of an illness caused by the radiation she worked with.", "She named polonium after her homeland, Poland.", "Her notebooks are still radioactive and are kept in lead boxes."],
  "Махатма Ганди": ["An Indian lawyer who in South Africa and then in India led a non-violent struggle against injustice. In 1930 he walked 385 km to the sea in the “Salt March”. India became independent in 1947, and the next year he was killed at 78.", "He was nominated five times for the Nobel Peace Prize but never received it.", "He spun the cloth for his own clothes."],
  "Орвил Райт": ["An American who with his brother Wilbur repaired bicycles in Dayton and built aeroplanes. On 17 December 1903 Orville piloted the first controlled powered flight: 12 seconds and 37 metres. He died at 76.", "Neil Armstrong took a piece of their aeroplane's fabric to the Moon.", "Neither of the brothers ever married."],
  "Гулиелмо Маркони": ["An Italian inventor from Bologna who created radio. In 1901 he sent a radio signal across the Atlantic. At 35 he received the Nobel Prize, and he died at 63.", "The survivors of the “Titanic” were rescued because the ship sent a call for help with his radio.", "When he died, radio stations all over the world fell silent for two minutes."],
  "Уинстън Чърчил": ["A British statesman born at Blenheim Palace. As prime minister he led Britain through the Second World War, promising “nothing but blood, toil, tears and sweat”. He received the Nobel Prize in Literature and died at 90.", "As a young reporter he escaped from captivity in the Boer War.", "He painted about 500 pictures."],
  "Томас Ман": ["A German writer from Lübeck, author of “Buddenbrooks” and “The Magic Mountain”. In 1929 he received the Nobel Prize. An opponent of the Nazis, he emigrated to the USA and died at 80 in Zurich.", "He wrote “Buddenbrooks” at just 25.", "His whole family wrote: his brother Heinrich and his children Klaus and Erika were writers too."],
  "Елин Пелин": ["A writer from the village of Baylovo, real name Dimitar Ivanov Stoyanov. A master of the short story about village life and author of the children's books about Yan Bibiyan. He died at 72 in Sofia.", "In the sequel Yan Bibiyan flies all the way to the Moon.", "For many years he worked as a librarian at the university library."],
  "Пейо Яворов": ["A poet from Chirpan, born Peyo Kracholov. He fought with the bands in Macedonia and wrote some of the most powerful Bulgarian poems of love and sorrow. After the tragic death of his wife Lora he lost his sight, and at 36 he took his own life.", "He took the name “Yavorov” from the sycamore tree, the yavor.", "He wrote the poem “Two Beautiful Eyes”, which became a famous song."],
  "Алберт Айнщайн": ["A physicist from Ulm, creator of the theory of relativity and the formula E = mc². In 1921 he received the Nobel Prize, and in 1933 he emigrated to the USA. He died at 76 in Princeton.", "It is not true that he was bad at maths; that is a myth.", "In 1952 he was offered the presidency of Israel, but turned it down."],
  "Александър Флеминг": ["A Scottish doctor and scientist. In 1928, back from holiday, he noticed mould in a forgotten dish where the bacteria around it had died, and so discovered penicillin. In 1945 he received the Nobel Prize, and he died at 73.", "Penicillin and the antibiotics after it have saved hundreds of millions of lives.", "Earlier he had found a germ-killing substance in his own nasal mucus."],
  "Пабло Пикасо": ["A Spanish painter from Málaga who created Cubism together with Braque. He painted “Guernica”, a cry against the bombing of a town. He made about 50,000 works and died at 91.", "His first word was “piz”, from “lápiz”, pencil.", "His full name contains more than 20 names of saints and relatives."],
  "Франц Кафка": ["A writer from Prague who wrote in German while working as a clerk in an insurance company. Author of “The Metamorphosis”, about a man who wakes one morning as a huge insect, and “The Trial”. He died at 40 of tuberculosis.", "He asked his friend Max Brod to burn all his manuscripts, but Brod published them.", "His name gave us the word “Kafkaesque”, for a senseless and frightening situation."],
  "Чарли Чаплин": ["An actor and director who grew up poor in London. He created the Tramp with the cane and bowler hat, the best-known character of silent film. In “The Great Dictator” he mocked Hitler. He died at 88 in Switzerland on Christmas Day.", "In 1978 his coffin was stolen for ransom and found a few weeks later.", "It is said he once entered a Chaplin look-alike contest and did not win."],
  "Агата Кристи": ["An English writer from Torquay, the best-selling novelist in history. She created the detectives Hercule Poirot and Miss Marple. Her play “The Mousetrap” has run without a break since 1952. She died at 85.", "In 1926 she vanished for 11 days, and nobody knows for sure what happened.", "She worked in a hospital pharmacy, which is why she knew so much about poisons."],
  "Дж. Р. Р. Толкин": ["Born in Bloemfontein, South Africa, a professor at Oxford. He wrote “The Hobbit” and “The Lord of the Rings” and invented whole languages for the elves. He died at 81.", "He scribbled the first line of “The Hobbit”, “In a hole in the ground there lived a hobbit”, on a blank page while marking exam papers.", "As a young man he fought at the Battle of the Somme in the First World War."],
  "Елисавета Багряна": ["A poet from Sofia, real name Elisaveta Belcheva. Her poems of freedom, the sea and womanhood made her the best-loved Bulgarian woman poet. She died at 97.", "Her pen name “Bagryana” comes from “bagren”, crimson.", "She was nominated for the Nobel Prize in Literature."],
  "Марлене Дитрих": ["A German actress and singer from Berlin who became a star with the film “The Blue Angel”. She refused to work for the Nazis, became an American and sang for soldiers at the front. She died at 90 in Paris.", "Soldiers on both sides of the front loved her song “Lili Marleen”.", "She shocked people by wearing trousers when that was not done by women."],
  "Уолт Дисни": ["An American animator from Chicago who created Mickey Mouse in 1928 and “Snow White”, the first full-length animated film. He opened Disneyland in 1955 and died at 65.", "He did Mickey Mouse's voice himself.", "He won 22 Oscars, more than anyone else."],
  "Джордж Оруел": ["An English writer born in India as Eric Blair. He wrote “Animal Farm” and “1984”, books against tyranny. He fought in the Spanish Civil War and was shot in the throat. He died at 46 of tuberculosis.", "The phrase “Big Brother is watching you” comes from “1984”.", "He wrote “1984” on a remote Scottish island, already gravely ill."],
  "Сергей Корольов": ["A Soviet rocket designer born in Zhytomyr. As chief designer he sent the first satellite into space in 1957 and Yuri Gagarin in 1961. He died at 59 during an operation.", "While he lived his name was a state secret; he was called only “the Chief Designer”.", "Before that he spent years in a Siberian prison camp, wrongly accused."],
  "Фрида Кало": ["A Mexican painter from Coyoacán. At 18 a bus crash left her in pain for life, and she began to paint in bed. Most of her pictures are self-portraits. She died at 47.", "Her home, the “Blue House”, is now a museum.", "Her last painting, a still life of watermelons, bears the words “Viva la vida”, “Long live life”."],
  "Никола Вапцаров": ["A poet from Bansko who went to naval school and worked as a ship's stoker and engineer. His only book of poems, “Motor Songs”, is about work and faith in people. He was shot at 32 for taking part in the resistance.", "Hours before his execution he wrote his last poem, “The fight is ruthlessly cruel”.", "A peak in Antarctica is named after him."],
  "Конрад Цузе": ["A German engineer from Berlin who in 1941 built the Z3, the first working programmable computer. He put his first machines together in his parents' living room. He died at 85.", "He also invented Plankalkül, the first high-level programming language.", "Besides engineering he was also a painter."],
  "Майка Тереза": ["Born in Skopje as Anjezë Bojaxhiu, a nun who gave her life to the poorest in Calcutta. She founded the order of the Missionaries of Charity. In 1979 she received the Nobel Peace Prize, and she died at 87; she has been declared a saint.", "She asked for the Nobel banquet to be cancelled and the money given to the poor.", "She wore a white sari with blue stripes, which became the sign of her order."],
  "Ванга": ["A Bulgarian clairvoyant from Strumica who went blind as a child after a storm. She lived in Petrich, where tens of thousands came to her, from villagers to statesmen. She died in 1996 at 85.", "The state paid her a salary as an employee of an institute studying her abilities.", "Many 'prophecies' circulate under her name that she never actually made."],
  "Алън Тюринг": ["An English mathematician, the father of computer science. He described the 'Turing machine', the idea behind the computer, and in the Second World War helped break the German Enigma cipher. Convicted for homosexuality, he died of cyanide poisoning at 41.", "He invented the 'Turing test': can a machine pass for a human in conversation?", "He ran a marathon in 2 hours 46 minutes and nearly made the Olympic team."],
  "Вернер фон Браун": ["A German rocket engineer. He built the V-2 rocket for Nazi Germany, and after the war was taken to the USA, where he designed the Saturn V that carried people to the Moon. He died in 1977 at 65.", "The V-2 was built by prisoners in terrible conditions: more people died making it than were killed by its strikes.", "As a boy he tied rockets to a toy wagon and sent it down a Berlin street, and the police picked him up."],
  "Роза Паркс": ["An African American seamstress from Montgomery, Alabama. In 1955 she refused to give up her bus seat to a white passenger and was arrested. The bus boycott that followed launched the civil rights movement. She died in 2005 at 92.", "The boycott lasted 381 days, until bus segregation was banned.", "After her death she lay in honour in the Capitol, the first woman to do so."],
  "Норман Борлоуг": ["An American agronomist, father of the 'Green Revolution'. He bred a short, hardy wheat that doubled and tripled harvests in Mexico, India and Pakistan. He received the Nobel Peace Prize in 1970 and died at 95.", "His work is thought to have saved about a billion people from starvation.", "He learned of his prize out in a field, where his wife drove to tell him."],
  "Нелсън Мандела": ["A South African fighter against apartheid. He spent 27 years in prison, and after his release in 1990 became South Africa's first Black president and reconciled the country. He died in 2013 at 95.", "He spent most of his prison years on Robben Island, breaking rocks in a quarry.", "He shared the 1993 Nobel Peace Prize with F. W. de Klerk, the president who released him."],
  "Йоан Павел II": ["Karol Wojtyła, the first Polish pope and the first non-Italian in 455 years. Pope from 1978 to 2005, he travelled the whole world and helped bring down communism in Eastern Europe. He died at 84.", "In 1981 he survived an assassination attempt and later forgave the gunman in prison.", "As a young man he was an actor and wrote plays."],
  "Софи Шол": ["A German student in Munich, a member of the 'White Rose' group that spread leaflets against Hitler. In 1943 she was caught throwing leaflets at the university and was beheaded at 21, together with her brother Hans.", "Only a few days passed between her arrest and her execution.", "The group's last leaflet was smuggled to England, and British planes dropped millions of copies over Germany."],
  "Мартин Лутър Кинг": ["An American pastor and leader of the civil rights movement. He preached non-violence and in 1963 gave the 'I Have a Dream' speech. He received the Nobel Peace Prize and was shot in 1968 at 39.", "At 35 he was the youngest Nobel Peace Prize winner up to then.", "He was born Michael; his father changed both their names after a trip to Germany."],
  "Ана Франк": ["A Jewish girl from Frankfurt whose family fled to Amsterdam. For two years she hid with them in a secret annex and kept a diary. In 1944 they were betrayed, and she died of typhus in Bergen-Belsen camp at 15.", "Her father was the only one of the eight in hiding to survive, and he published the diary.", "She got the diary for her 13th birthday and called it 'Kitty'."],
  "Нийл Армстронг": ["An American astronaut and test pilot. On 20 July 1969, with Apollo 11, he became the first person to walk on the Moon. He died in 2012 at 82.", "His first words there: 'One small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.'", "His footprints are probably still there, since there is no wind on the Moon."],
  "Юрий Гагарин": ["A Soviet pilot, son of collective farm workers. On 12 April 1961, in Vostok 1, he became the first human in space, circling the Earth in 108 minutes. He died in a fighter training flight in 1968 at 34.", "At lift-off he said 'Poyekhali!', 'Let's go!'", "He landed by parachute in a field and startled a farm woman and her granddaughter."],
  "Елвис Пресли": ["An American singer from Mississippi, the 'King of Rock and Roll'. From truck driver he became the biggest star of the 1950s with songs like 'Hound Dog'. He died at his home, Graceland, in 1977 at 42.", "He had a twin brother, Jesse, who died at birth.", "He has sold over half a billion records."],
  "Джон Ленън": ["An English musician from Liverpool, founder of the Beatles. After the band split he wrote 'Imagine' and campaigned for peace with Yoko Ono. He was shot outside his New York home in 1980 at 40.", "His killer had got his autograph just hours earlier.", "His teachers wrote that he was 'on the road to failure'."],
  "Стивън Хокинг": ["A British physicist who studied black holes and the beginning of the universe. At 21 he got ALS and was given two years to live, but he lived 55 more, speaking through a computer. He died in 2018 at 76.", "His book 'A Brief History of Time' sold over 10 million copies.", "He was born exactly 300 years after Galileo died, and died on Einstein's birthday."],
  "Мохамед Али": ["An American boxer, born Cassius Clay. Three times world heavyweight champion, known for his speed and the line 'Float like a butterfly, sting like a bee'. He refused to fight in Vietnam. He died in 2016 at 74.", "He took up boxing at 12 because his bike was stolen and he wanted to beat up the thief.", "For refusing to serve he lost his title and did not fight for almost four years."],
  "Фреди Меркюри": ["Singer of the British band Queen, born Farrokh Bulsara in Zanzibar. He wrote 'Bohemian Rhapsody' and 'We Are the Champions' and had a four-octave voice. He died of AIDS in 1991 at 45.", "He designed the band's crest himself, having studied graphic design.", "Their 1985 Live Aid set is often called the greatest live performance ever."],
  "Стив Джобс": ["An American entrepreneur who founded Apple with Steve Wozniak in a garage. Thrown out of his own company, he came back and created the iPod, iPhone and iPad. He died of pancreatic cancer in 2011 at 56.", "He was adopted as a baby; his biological father was from Syria.", "He bought a small studio that became Pixar and made 'Toy Story'."],
  "Хатшепсут": ["An Egyptian queen who declared herself pharaoh and ruled for about 20 years of peace and wealth. She built the splendid temple at Deir el-Bahari and sent an expedition to the distant land of Punt. She died at about 49.", "Her statues often show her wearing the false pharaoh's beard.", "After her death someone carefully erased her name and images from the monuments."],
  "Кир Велики": ["Founder of the Persian Empire, the largest yet, from the Mediterranean to India. He conquered Media, Lydia and Babylon, but treated conquered peoples tolerantly. He fell in battle around 530 BC at about 70.", "He let the Jews return from the Babylonian exile and rebuild their temple.", "A clay cylinder with his words is sometimes called 'the first charter of human rights'."],
  "Дарий I": ["A Persian king who organised the vast empire: he split it into provinces, brought in a common coin and built the 2,700 km Royal Road. He invaded Greece but was beaten at Marathon. He died in 486 BC at 64.", "On the Royal Road couriers covered in a week what took others three months on foot.", "He built a dazzling new city, Persepolis."],
  "Сун Дзъ": ["A Chinese general and thinker credited with the book 'The Art of War'. He served the state of Wu. Very little is known of his life; he is thought to have died at about 48.", "His best-known idea: the best victory is to defeat the enemy without fighting.", "Today his book is read by business leaders and sports coaches."],
  "Темистокъл": ["An Athenian politician and general. He persuaded Athens to build a fleet with silver from its mines, and with it beat the Persians at Salamis in 480 BC. Later he was exiled from Athens and died in Persia at 65.", "The Athenians spent the money from a new silver vein on ships instead of sharing it out.", "In the end he served the very Persian king whose father he had defeated."],
  "Тукидид": ["An Athenian historian and general. He wrote about the Peloponnesian War between Athens and Sparta, looking for real causes instead of the will of the gods. He died around 400 BC at about 60.", "He survived the plague of Athens himself and described it in detail.", "He wrote the book in exile, after failing as a commander to save a city."],
  "Аристофан": ["An Athenian writer of comedies. In plays like 'The Clouds', 'The Birds' and 'Lysistrata' he mocked politicians, philosophers and war. He died around 386 BC at about 60.", "In 'Lysistrata' the women stop a war by refusing their husbands.", "In 'The Clouds' he mocks Socrates, who sits in a basket hanging in the air."],
  "Диоген": ["A Greek Cynic philosopher who lived in poverty and ignored every custom. He slept in a large clay jar in Athens and taught that happiness lies in a simple life. He died in Corinth at about 89.", "In broad daylight he walked around with a lamp, saying he was 'looking for a human being'.", "When Alexander the Great asked what he wanted, Diogenes said: 'Stand aside, you're blocking my sun.'"],
  "Ератостен": ["A Greek scholar who ran the Library of Alexandria. He measured the Earth's circumference from shadows in two cities and came very close to the real value. He died around 194 BC at 82.", "He invented the 'sieve of Eratosthenes' for finding prime numbers.", "He was the first to use the word 'geography'."],
  "Сципион Африкански": ["A Roman general who beat Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC and ended the Second Punic War. Later attacked by political enemies, he retired to his estate, where he died at 53.", "He commanded an army at just 25, well below the legal age.", "He reportedly had his tomb say: 'Ungrateful fatherland, you will not even have my bones.'"],
  "Помпей Велики": ["A Roman general who cleared the Mediterranean of pirates and conquered the East. First Julius Caesar's ally and then his enemy, he lost the civil war and fled to Egypt, where he was killed in 48 BC at 57.", "He dealt with the pirates in about three months.", "He built Rome's first stone theatre; Caesar was later killed in its complex."],
  "Арминий": ["A Germanic chief of the Cherusci who had served in the Roman army. In AD 9, in the Teutoburg Forest, he lured three Roman legions into an ambush and destroyed them. He was killed by his own relatives at about 38.", "After the defeat Emperor Augustus cried: 'Varus, give me back my legions!'", "In Germany he is called Hermann, and his monument is over 50 metres tall."],
  "Сенека": ["A Roman Stoic philosopher, writer and tutor to Emperor Nero. He wrote letters on calm, time and death that are still read today. Nero accused him of a plot and ordered him to kill himself; he died in AD 65 at 68.", "For a while he was the most powerful man in Rome next to the young Nero.", "He said: 'It is not that we have little time, but that we waste much of it.'"],
  "Плиний Стари": ["A Roman scholar, writer and fleet commander. He wrote the 'Natural History', a vast encyclopaedia of everything then known. He died in AD 79 at 56, trying to rescue people during the eruption of Vesuvius.", "He worked even in the bath and at meals, with someone reading aloud to him.", "His nephew Pliny the Younger described the eruption in a letter, the first detailed account of a volcano."],
  "Будика": ["Queen of the British Iceni tribe. After the Romans seized her lands and shamed her family, in AD 60 she led a revolt and burned Londinium. Defeated by the Roman army, she died at about 31, probably by taking poison.", "Her rebels burned three cities, among them the future London.", "Her bronze statue in a war chariot stands next to the British Parliament."],
  "Цай Лун": ["A Chinese court official of the Han dynasty. Around 105 he improved and described paper-making from bark, old rags and fishing nets. Caught up in palace intrigue, he took his own life at about 71.", "Before paper the Chinese wrote on bamboo strips and costly silk.", "The secret of paper reached Europe only about a thousand years later."],
  "Траян": ["A Roman emperor born in Spain. Under him the empire was at its largest: he conquered Dacia and reached the Persian Gulf. He built markets, bridges and roads. He died in 117 at 63.", "Trajan's Column in Rome tells the Dacian war in over 2,500 figures.", "In today's Bulgaria the cities of Nicopolis ad Istrum and Ulpia Oescus bear his name."],
  "Адриан": ["A Roman emperor who stopped conquering and strengthened the borders. He travelled the whole empire and built Hadrian's Wall in Britain and the Pantheon in Rome. He died in 138 at 62.", "He was the first emperor with a beard, a fashion followed for a century.", "Edirne (Adrianople) was founded by him and bears his name."],
  "Гален": ["A Greek doctor from Pergamon who worked in Rome as physician to emperors. He studied anatomy by dissecting animals and wrote hundreds of works. His teachings ruled medicine for almost 1,400 years. He died at about 87.", "As a young man he treated wounded gladiators.", "He showed that arteries carry blood, not air, as had been believed."],
  "Диоклециан": ["A Roman emperor, son of a freed slave. He split the empire among four rulers, reformed taxes and the army, and harshly persecuted Christians. In 305 he stepped down and grew cabbages at his palace in Split. He died at about 66.", "He was the only Roman emperor to retire of his own will.", "Asked to return to power, he said that if they could see his cabbages, they wouldn't ask."],
  "Свети Николай Чудотворец": ["Bishop of Myra in Asia Minor, known for his kindness. He secretly helped the poor and defended the innocent. He died around 343 at about 73 and became one of the most loved saints, patron of sailors and fishermen.", "He secretly tossed bags of gold into a poor father's home so his daughters could marry; Santa Claus comes from him.", "In Bulgaria his feast day is celebrated with fish, usually carp."],
  "Свети Патрик": ["The man who brought Christianity to Ireland, born in Roman Britain. At 16 he was kidnapped by pirates and spent six years herding sheep as a slave in Ireland before escaping. He returned as a missionary and converted the Irish. He died at about 76.", "He explained the Holy Trinity with a three-leaf shamrock, which became Ireland's symbol.", "His day, 17 March, is celebrated around the world in green."],
  "Теодорих Велики": ["King of the Ostrogoths, who spent his childhood as a hostage in Constantinople. He conquered Italy and ruled it peacefully for over 30 years, keeping Roman law. He died in Ravenna in 526 at 72.", "As a young man he lived and fought in today's Bulgaria too, around Novae near Svishtov.", "In German legend he is the hero Dietrich von Bern."],
  "Хлодвиг I": ["King of the Franks, who united Gaul and founded the Frankish state. Around 500 he became a Christian and so won the Church's support. He made Paris his capital and died in 511 at 45.", "The name Clovis later became Louis, the name of 18 French kings.", "He was baptised together with 3,000 of his warriors."],
  "Свети Бенедикт": ["An Italian monk who founded the monastery of Monte Cassino. He wrote the 'Rule', guidelines for monks' life of prayer and work, followed by thousands of monasteries. He died around 547 at 67 and is patron saint of Europe.", "His monks' motto is 'Ora et labora', 'Pray and work'.", "As a young man he lived alone in a cave for three years."],
  "Императрица Теодора": ["Byzantine empress, wife of Justinian. From actress and daughter of a bear-keeper she became one of the most powerful women in history. During the Nika riots she persuaded her husband not to flee. She died in 548 at 48.", "She said that 'royal purple makes a fine burial shroud': better to die an emperor than to run.", "She pushed through laws protecting women."],
  "Хан Кубрат": ["A ruler of the Dulo clan who united the Bulgars and around 632 founded Old Great Bulgaria by the Black and Azov Seas. As a young man he lived in Constantinople and was a friend of Emperor Heraclius. He died around 665 at about 60.", "Legend says that before dying he gave his sons a bundle of sticks: together they won't break, one by one they snap easily.", "His grave was probably found at Pereshchepyna in Ukraine, with over 20 kg of gold."],
  "Ву Дзътиен": ["The only woman in Chinese history to rule as emperor in her own name. From concubine she became the emperor's wife, then founded her own dynasty. She died in 705 at 81.", "She promoted the civil service exams, so people without noble birth could rise to power.", "She invented over ten new Chinese characters, including one for her own name."],
  "Хан Тервел": ["A Bulgarian khan, son of Asparuh. In 705 he helped the deposed emperor Justinian II regain his throne and received the title 'Caesar' and land. In 717–718 he helped Byzantium crush the Arabs before Constantinople. He died around 721 at about 46.", "He was the first foreigner to receive the Byzantine title of Caesar.", "He is called 'the saviour of Europe' for stopping the Arabs at Constantinople."],
  "Харун ар-Рашид": ["Abbasid caliph in Baghdad when the caliphate was at its height. He supported scholars, poets and trade, and Baghdad became the largest city in the world. He died in 809 at 46.", "He appears in the 'Thousand and One Nights', walking through Baghdad in disguise.", "He sent Charlemagne an elephant named Abul-Abbas and an elaborate water clock."],
  "Хан Омуртаг": ["A Bulgarian khan, son of Krum. He made a 30-year peace with Byzantium and turned to building palaces, fortresses and bridges, leaving many inscriptions in Greek. He died around 831 at about 41.", "One of his inscriptions says: 'A man dies, even if he lives well, and another is born.'", "He built a new palace on the Danube and a famous fortified residence near Pliska."],
  "Свети Наум Охридски": ["A disciple of Cyril and Methodius. After Methodius died he came to Bulgaria, worked in Pliska and Preslav, then in Ohrid. He founded a monastery on the shore of Lake Ohrid and died there in 910 at about 80.", "Legend says he harnessed a bear to his plough after it ate his ox.", "The monastery of Saint Naum is still a place of pilgrimage."],
  "Свети Климент Охридски": ["A disciple of Cyril and Methodius and one of the greatest Bulgarian men of letters. Sent to Kutmichevitsa (Ohrid), he taught over 3,500 pupils and wrote many sermons. He became the first bishop to preach in Bulgarian and died in 916 at about 76.", "He is thought to have created Cyrillic by simplifying Glagolitic and bringing it closer to the Greek alphabet.", "Sofia University bears his name."],
  "Цар Самуил": ["A Bulgarian tsar who defended the state for decades against Emperor Basil II, ruling from Ohrid. In 1014 his army was crushed at Belasitsa and Basil blinded the thousands of prisoners. Samuel died of a stroke soon after, at 56.", "One man in every hundred was left with one eye to lead the rest home.", "Basil II earned the nickname 'the Bulgar Slayer'."],
  "Лейф Ериксон": ["An Icelandic Viking, son of Erik the Red. Around 1000 he reached North America, nearly 500 years before Columbus, and called it Vinland. He died around 1020 at about 50.", "Remains of a Viking settlement have been found in Newfoundland, Canada.", "Vinland means 'wine land': they found wild grapes there."],
  "Уилям Завоевателя": ["Duke of Normandy who won at Hastings in 1066 and became King of England. He built castles like the Tower of London and recorded the whole country in the Domesday Book. He died in 1087 at 59.", "After him English filled up with French words.", "At his funeral his body would not fit into the tomb and burst."],
  "Фридрих Барбароса": ["A German emperor of the Hohenstaufen family, called 'Red Beard'. He fought long in Italy and led the Third Crusade. He drowned in a river in Asia Minor in 1190 at 68.", "On the way to the Holy Land his army passed through Bulgarian lands, near Plovdiv.", "Legend says he sleeps in the Kyffhäuser mountain and will wake when Germany needs him."],
  "Авероес": ["An Arab philosopher, doctor and judge from Córdoba in Andalusia. He wrote detailed commentaries on Aristotle through which Europe got to know him again. He fell from favour but was pardoned, and died in Marrakesh in 1198 at 72.", "In Europe he was known simply as 'the Commentator'.", "Raphael painted him among the Greek philosophers in 'The School of Athens'."],
  "Маймонид": ["A Jewish philosopher, doctor and scholar of law, born in Córdoba. His family fled persecution, and he settled in Cairo and became physician to Sultan Saladin. He died in 1204 at 66.", "People say: 'From Moses to Moses there was none like Moses.'", "He also wrote books on healthy eating and on asthma."],
  "Фибоначи": ["An Italian mathematician from Pisa who grew up in North Africa. With his 1202 'Book of Calculation' he brought Arabic numerals and zero to Europe. He died around 1250 at about 80.", "His puzzle about breeding rabbits gives the famous Fibonacci numbers: 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8…", "These numbers show up in sunflowers, pine cones and shells."],
  "Цар Калоян": ["A Bulgarian tsar, brother of Asen and Peter. He got a crown from the Pope, and in 1205 at Adrianople crushed the knights of the Fourth Crusade and captured the Latin emperor Baldwin. He was killed besieging Thessaloniki in 1207 at 37.", "He called himself 'Roman Slayer', an answer to Basil II's nickname.", "His grave was probably found in the Church of the Forty Martyrs in Veliko Tarnovo."],
  "Валтер фон дер Фогелвайде": ["The most famous German Minnesinger, a medieval poet and singer of love. He travelled between the courts of princes and emperors, singing of love, politics and the meaning of life. He died around 1230 at about 60.", "The only record of him outside his songs is a bill: a bishop paid for his fur coat.", "Legend says he left money for the birds on his grave to be fed grain every day."],
  "Цар Иван Асен II": ["A Bulgarian tsar under whom the Second Bulgarian Empire was at its largest, reaching three seas. In 1230 he defeated Theodore Komnenos of Epirus at Klokotnitsa, yet let the prisoners go. He died in 1241 at about 51.", "He let the captured soldiers go home, which earned him respect from his people and his enemies.", "An inscription in the Church of the Forty Martyrs tells of his victory."],
  "Албертус Магнус": ["A German Dominican friar, philosopher and naturalist, teacher of Thomas Aquinas. He studied plants, animals, minerals and chemistry and wrote about almost everything. He died in Cologne in 1280 at about 80.", "He was the first to describe pure arsenic.", "He was called 'Doctor Universalis' because he knew about everything."],
  "Кублай хан": ["A Mongol khan, grandson of Genghis Khan. He conquered all of China, founded the Yuan dynasty and made Beijing his capital. He tried twice to invade Japan, but storms wrecked his fleet. He died in 1294 at 78.", "Marco Polo served at his court for about 17 years.", "The Japanese called the storms that saved them 'kamikaze', 'divine wind'."],
  "Майстер Екхарт": ["A German Dominican friar, theologian and mystic. He preached in German instead of Latin and taught that God can be born in every person's soul. Accused of heresy, he died around 1328 at about 68, before the verdict.", "He helped make German a language for philosophy too.", "Neither the place nor the date of his death is known."],
  "Джото": ["An Italian painter and architect from Florence. He painted people with real feelings and depth instead of the flat faces of icons, the start of Renaissance art. He designed the bell tower of Florence Cathedral. He died in 1337 at 70.", "As a boy, the story goes, he drew a sheep on a rock so well that a painter took him on.", "To prove his skill he drew a perfect circle with one stroke."],
  "Манса Муса": ["Ruler of the gold-rich Mali Empire in West Africa, probably the richest person in history. He built mosques and schools in Timbuktu. He died around 1337 at about 57.", "On his way to Mecca he gave out so much gold in Cairo that its price dropped for years.", "A 14th-century European map shows him with a gold crown and a gold nugget in hand."],
  "Ибн Батута": ["A Moroccan who set out on pilgrimage to Mecca at 21 and came home 24 years later. He covered about 120,000 km, from North Africa to China, India and East Africa. He died around 1369 at 65.", "He travelled three times as far as Marco Polo.", "He served as a judge in Delhi and in the Maldives."],
  "Франческо Петрарка": ["An Italian poet and scholar, the 'father of humanism'. He wrote sonnets to Laura, whom he saw in a church and loved without return all his life. He hunted down and copied ancient manuscripts. He died in 1374 at 69.", "He was the first to call the Middle Ages the 'Dark Ages'.", "He climbed Mont Ventoux just for the view, which was unusual for his time."],
  "Патриарх Евтимий": ["The last patriarch of the Tarnovo kingdom and a great man of letters. He reformed spelling and corrected the church books. In 1393 he led the defence of Tarnovo against the Ottomans. After the city fell he was exiled and died around 1402 at about 77.", "Tradition says that when the Turks broke in he faced them without fear and they did not kill him.", "His pupils carried the Tarnovo literary school to Serbia and Russia."],
  "Тимур": ["A Turco-Mongol conqueror called Tamerlane, 'Timur the Lame'. He built a huge empire from India to Asia Minor, and his capital Samarkand was adorned with magnificent buildings. He died in 1405 at 68, on his way to attack China.", "In 1402 he crushed and captured the Ottoman sultan Bayezid I, delaying the fall of Constantinople by 50 years.", "Scientists who opened his tomb in 1941 confirmed that he limped."],
  "Джефри Чосър": ["An English poet, the 'father of English literature'. He wrote 'The Canterbury Tales', stories told by pilgrims on the road, and wrote in English when the court spoke French. He died in 1400 at about 57.", "He worked as a customs official and a diplomat.", "He was the first poet buried in Westminster's Poets' Corner."],
  "Цар Иван Шишман": ["The last tsar of the Tarnovo kingdom. He tried to resist the Ottomans, but Tarnovo fell in 1393. He died in 1395 at about 45, by tradition in captivity or in battle near Nikopol.", "Many folk songs and legends tell of him.", "Many places in Bulgaria bear his name: 'Shishman's fortress', 'Shishman's gates'."],
  "Джън Хъ": ["A Chinese admiral and eunuch. He led seven huge sea voyages to India, Arabia and East Africa in ships far bigger than Columbus's. He died around 1433 at about 62, probably at sea.", "His fleets had up to 300 ships and about 28,000 men.", "He brought a giraffe from Africa for the emperor."],
  "Ян Хус": ["A Czech priest and professor in Prague who criticised the wealth and sins of the Church. Called to the Council of Constance with a promise of safety, he was arrested and burned at the stake in 1415 at 43.", "His death sparked the Hussite Wars in Bohemia.", "He wrote and preached in Czech and simplified Czech spelling."],
  "Влад Цепеш": ["A Wallachian prince who fought the Ottomans, notorious for impaling his enemies on stakes. He was killed in battle in 1476 at 45.", "The name 'Dracula' comes from his father, a member of the Order of the Dragon.", "The Irish writer Bram Stoker used his name for the vampire Count Dracula."],
  "Мехмед II Завоевателя": ["An Ottoman sultan who took Constantinople in 1453 at 21, ending the Byzantine Empire. He made the city his capital and built Topkapı Palace. He died in 1481 at 49.", "He moved his ships overland on greased planks to get round the chain across the Golden Horn.", "He spoke several languages and invited an Italian painter to paint his portrait."],
  "Лукас Кранах Старши": ["A German Renaissance painter, court painter in Saxony. He painted portraits, including the best-known ones of his friend Martin Luther. He also owned a pharmacy and a printing press. He died in 1553 at 81.", "He was a witness at Luther's wedding.", "He signed his pictures with a winged snake."],
  "Парацелз": ["A Swiss doctor and alchemist. He rejected old teachings, wrote in German and used chemical substances as medicines. He travelled Europe quarrelling with colleagues. He died in Salzburg in 1541 at 47.", "He said: 'All things are poison; only the dose makes a thing not a poison.'", "He publicly burned the books of Avicenna to show it was time for new medicine."],
  "Ханс Холбайн Младши": ["A German painter from Augsburg, one of the greatest portraitists. He worked in Basel and then in England as court painter to Henry VIII. He died in London in 1543 at 46, probably of the plague.", "His painting 'The Ambassadors' hides a stretched skull that looks right only from the side.", "Henry VIII chose a wife from his portrait and was disappointed when he met her."],
  "Карл V": ["Holy Roman Emperor and King of Spain from the House of Habsburg. He ruled so many lands in Europe and America that 'the sun never set' on them. He fought France, the Ottomans and the Protestants. He abdicated and died in a monastery in 1558 at 58.", "He supposedly said he spoke Spanish to God, Italian to women, French to men and German to his horse.", "He faced Luther at the Diet of Worms."],
  "Готфрид Вилхелм Лайбниц": ["A German philosopher and mathematician from Leipzig, one of the last people who knew everything. He invented calculus independently of Newton and built a mechanical calculator. He died in Hanover in 1716 at 70, almost forgotten.", "He described the binary system of 0 and 1 on which today's computers run.", "Only his secretary came to his funeral."],
  "Мария Сибила Мериан": ["A German artist and naturalist from Frankfurt. She painted insects and plants and showed how a caterpillar becomes a butterfly. At 52 she sailed with her daughter to Suriname to study tropical insects. She died in Amsterdam in 1717 at 69.", "At the time many people believed insects were born from mud.", "Her portrait was on the German 500-mark banknote."],
  "Барон Мюнхаузен": ["A German nobleman who served as an officer in the Russian army against the Turks. Back on his estate near Hanover he told guests incredible tales of his adventures. He died in 1797 at 76.", "In the tales he rides a cannonball and pulls himself out of a swamp by his own hair.", "Others published his stories without his consent, which made him angry."],
  "Готхолд Ефраим Лесинг": ["A German writer and playwright of the Enlightenment. In the play 'Nathan the Wise' he called for tolerance between Christians, Jews and Muslims. He worked as a librarian in Wolfenbüttel. He died in 1781 at 52.", "'Nathan the Wise' contains the famous parable of the three rings.", "He was one of the first Germans to try to live from writing alone."],
  "Йозеф Хайдн": ["An Austrian composer, 'father of the symphony and the string quartet'. For almost 30 years he was court musician to the Esterházy princes. He wrote over 100 symphonies and taught Beethoven. He died in Vienna in 1809 at 77.", "His tune for the emperor is today Germany's national anthem.", "In the 'Farewell' Symphony the players blow out their candles and leave one by one, a hint to the prince that they wanted a holiday."],
  "Софроний Врачански": ["A Bulgarian cleric and writer, born Stoyko Vladislavov in Kotel. He copied Paisius's 'Slavonic-Bulgarian History' and became bishop of Vratsa. He wrote the 'Nedelnik' (1806), the first printed book in modern Bulgarian. He died in Bucharest in 1813 at 74.", "He also wrote 'The Life and Sufferings of Sinful Sophronius', the first Bulgarian autobiography.", "He helped the Russian army and wrote appeals to the Bulgarians."],
  "Каролайн Хершел": ["A German astronomer from Hanover, sister of William Herschel. She joined her brother in England as a singer but became his assistant and discovered eight comets herself. She returned to Hanover and died in 1848 at 97.", "She was the first woman paid a salary by the king for scientific work.", "Typhus as a child left her only 1.30 m tall."],
  "Георг Вилхелм Фридрих Хегел": ["A German philosopher from Stuttgart. He taught that history moves forward through contradictions and that the world is the unfolding of spirit. He taught in Berlin and was the most influential philosopher of his time. He died of cholera in 1831 at 61.", "He finished his 'Phenomenology of Spirit' as Napoleon entered Jena.", "He had been a student friend of the poet Hölderlin."],
  "Фридрих Хьолдерлин": ["A German poet from Swabia who wrote soaring verse about ancient Greece and nature. Around 36 he fell mentally ill and spent the last 36 years of his life in a tower in Tübingen with a carpenter's family. He died in 1843 at 73.", "Hölderlin's tower in Tübingen is now a museum.", "He signed his late poems with made-up names such as 'Scardanelli'."],
  "Каспар Давид Фридрих": ["A German Romantic painter from Greifswald. He painted lonely figures before mists, seas and mountains. At the end of his life he was forgotten and poor. He died in Dresden in 1840 at 65.", "His most famous painting is 'Wanderer above the Sea of Fog'.", "As a boy, his brother drowned while saving him when they were skating on ice."],
  "Карл Дрез": ["A German inventor from Karlsruhe. In 1817 he built the 'running machine', a wooden two-wheeler without pedals, ancestor of the bicycle. He also invented a typewriter. He died poor in 1851 at 66.", "He invented it after the 'Year Without a Summer' killed many horses, so transport without them was needed.", "He also invented a machine to write down piano music."],
  "Вилхелм Грим": ["A German linguist and collector of fairy tales, the younger of the Brothers Grimm. With his brother Jacob he collected the 'Children's and Household Tales': 'Snow White', 'Cinderella', 'Hansel and Gretel'. They also began the vast German Dictionary. He died in Berlin in 1859 at 73.", "Wilhelm was the one who retold the tales more gently and beautifully for children.", "The dictionary the brothers started was only finished in 1961."],
  "Артур Шопенхауер": ["A German philosopher from Danzig, known for his pessimism. He taught that the world is driven by a blind will and life is suffering, eased by art and compassion. Recognition came only in old age. He died in Frankfurt in 1860 at 72.", "He scheduled his lectures at the same time as Hegel's, and almost nobody came to his.", "He lived with a series of poodles, all called Atma."],
  "Васил Априлов": ["A Bulgarian merchant and benefactor from Gabrovo who lived in Odessa. In 1835, with Nikola Palauzov, he founded in Gabrovo the first secular Bulgarian school. He died in Galați in 1847 at 58.", "As a young man he thought of himself as Greek; a book by Yuri Venelin turned him back to his Bulgarian roots.", "Gabrovo's Aprilov High School bears his name."],
  "Неофит Рилски": ["A Bulgarian monk, teacher and scholar from Bansko, the 'patriarch of Bulgarian education'. He wrote the first Bulgarian grammar (1835) and was the first teacher at the Aprilov school in Gabrovo. He spent his last years in the Rila Monastery, where he died in 1881 at 88.", "He spent 30 years on a large dictionary of Bulgarian.", "He translated the New Testament into spoken Bulgarian."],
  "Хайнрих Хайне": ["A German poet from Düsseldorf with Jewish roots. He wrote tender love poems as well as sharp satire. Because of censorship he lived in Paris, where he spent his last eight years bedridden. He died in 1856 at 58.", "His poem 'Lorelei' became a folk song.", "He wrote, prophetically: 'Where they burn books, they will in the end burn people too.'"],
  "Петър Берон": ["A Bulgarian scholar and educator from Kotel. In 1824 he published the 'Primer with Various Instructions', the 'Fish Primer', the first secular Bulgarian schoolbook. He later became a doctor and wrote on physics and philosophy. He was murdered in Craiova in 1871 at 72.", "It is called the 'Fish Primer' because of the dolphin on its last page.", "He left his fortune to Bulgarian schools."],
  "Кольо Фичето": ["A self-taught Bulgarian master builder of the National Revival from Dryanovo. He built bridges, churches and buildings: the bridge at Byala, the covered bridge in Lovech, the konak in Tarnovo. He died in 1881 at 81.", "The nickname 'Ficheto' means 'the little one': he was short.", "His bridge at Byala is 276 metres long."],
  "Захари Зограф": ["A Bulgarian icon painter of the National Revival from Samokov. He painted murals in the Rila, Bachkovo and Troyan monasteries. He also painted ordinary people and himself, which was new. He died of typhus in 1853 at 43.", "He painted the first Bulgarian self-portrait.", "In the Troyan Monastery he painted the Last Judgement with people in the clothes of his day."],
  "Роберт Шуман": ["A German Romantic composer from Zwickau. He wanted to be a pianist but injured his hand and turned to composing. He married the pianist Clara Wieck. Suffering from severe depression, he threw himself into the Rhine and died in an asylum in 1856 at 46.", "He wrote over a hundred songs in a single year, the year of his wedding.", "He discovered and championed the young Brahms."],
  "Баба Тонка": ["Tonka Obretenova from Ruse, 'mother of the revolutionaries'. Her home was a secret base for the committees, hiding rebels and weapons. Her sons joined the struggle, and two of them died. She died in 1893 at 81.", "She carried weapons and letters hidden under her clothes past Turkish guards.", "Her daughter Petrana was a revolutionary too."],
  "Вернер фон Сименс": ["A German inventor and industrialist. He founded the Siemens company, built telegraph lines across whole continents and found out how to make powerful electric generators. He died in 1892 at 75.", "He built the first electric tram and the first electric lift.", "The unit of electrical conductance is called the 'siemens'."],
  "Евлоги Георгиев": ["A Bulgarian merchant and banker from Karlovo who lived in Bucharest with his brother Hristo Georgiev. He donated a huge sum and land to found Sofia University. He died in Bucharest in 1897 at 78.", "Sofia University's main building stands on the land the brothers gave, with their statues on the front.", "He gave money to the revolutionaries but opposed an armed uprising."],
  "Клара Шуман": ["A German pianist and composer from Leipzig, a child prodigy. She married Robert Schumann against her father's will, had eight children and kept giving concerts across Europe. She died in 1896 at 76.", "She and Brahms were close friends all their lives.", "Her portrait was on the German 100-mark banknote."],
  "Теодор Фонтане": ["A German writer from Neuruppin, a pharmacist by trade. He worked as a journalist and war correspondent and wrote about his travels through Brandenburg. He wrote his best novels, like 'Effi Briest', after 60. He died in Berlin in 1898 at 78.", "As a correspondent in 1870 he was arrested by the French as a spy.", "His ballad of Herr von Ribbeck and his pears is learned in German schools."],
  "Добри Чинтулов": ["A Bulgarian poet and teacher from Sliven. He wrote rebel verses that became songs sung by the whole nation. He taught in Sliven and died there in 1886 at 64.", "His song 'Rise, rise, Balkan hero' was sung by the revolutionaries.", "His poems spread by word of mouth without being printed."],
  "Хайнрих Шлиман": ["A German merchant and archaeologist. He got rich from trade and the gold rush, then went looking for Troy using Homer's verses and found it in Turkey. He also dug at Mycenae. He died in Naples in 1890 at 68.", "He smuggled out 'Priam's Treasure' and put the jewels on his wife.", "He spoke over ten languages, learning each in a few weeks."],
  "Найден Геров": ["A Bulgarian man of the Revival, teacher and linguist from Koprivshtitsa. He created the 'Dictionary of the Bulgarian Language', a huge work with over 70,000 words. For many years he was Russian consul in Plovdiv. He died in 1900 at 77.", "While still a student he wrote the first Bulgarian narrative poem.", "As consul he helped many Bulgarians escape persecution."],
  "Йохан Щраус (син)": ["An Austrian composer, the 'Waltz King'. He wrote over 500 waltzes, polkas and operettas, among them 'The Blue Danube' and 'Die Fledermaus'. He toured Europe and America with his orchestra. He died in Vienna in 1899 at 73.", "His father, also a composer, did not want his son to become a musician.", "'The Blue Danube' is played in Vienna every New Year."],
  "Петко Славейков": ["A Bulgarian poet, journalist and politician from Tarnovo. He published newspapers, collected thousands of folk songs and proverbs and fought for an independent Bulgarian church. After the Liberation he was a minister. He died in 1895 at 67.", "He was the father of the poet Pencho Slaveykov.", "He published the newspapers 'Gayda' and 'Makedonia'."],
  "Йохана Шпири": ["A Swiss writer from a village near Zurich. She wrote 'Heidi', the story of a girl living with her grandfather in the Alps. She died in Zurich in 1901 at 74.", "'Heidi' has been translated into about 50 languages.", "Her first book came out without her name, only her initials."],
  "Панайот Хитов": ["A Bulgarian haidut and voivode from Sliven. He led bands in the Balkan Mountains, and in 1867 led a band across them with Vasil Levski as standard-bearer. He lived to see the Liberation and died in Ruse in 1918 at 87.", "He wrote memoirs, 'How I Became a Haidut'.", "Levski served as his standard-bearer."],
  "Филип Тотю": ["A Bulgarian haidut and voivode from the village of Gurtsi (today Vishovgrad). In 1867 he led a band from Romania into the Balkan Mountains alongside Panayot Hitov. He lived to see the Liberation and died in 1907 at 77.", "Folk songs are sung about him.", "He was one of the few rebel leaders who lived to see the Liberation."],
  "Франц Йосиф I": ["Emperor of Austria and King of Hungary who ruled for 68 years, from 1848 to 1916. He lived through lost wars, his son's death and the murder of his wife Sisi. After his heir was assassinated he declared war on Serbia, starting the First World War. He died in 1916 at 86.", "He became emperor at 18.", "He slept on an iron soldier's bed and rose every day at 4 a.m."],
  "Николаус Ото": ["A self-taught German inventor. In 1876 he created the four-stroke internal combustion engine that powers most cars. He died in Cologne in 1891 at 58.", "Before that he was a travelling salesman of sugar and groceries.", "The four-stroke cycle is still called the 'Otto cycle'."],
  "Вилхелм Буш": ["A German poet and artist, a father of the comic strip. He created 'Max and Moritz', a story in pictures and rhyme about two naughty boys. He lived quietly in the country and died in 1908 at 75.", "He sold the rights to 'Max and Moritz' for a small sum, and the publisher got rich.", "As a child he lived with his uncle, a pastor, and did not see his parents for three years."],
  "Йоханес Брамс": ["A German composer from Hamburg. As a boy he played piano in harbour taverns. He later settled in Vienna and wrote symphonies, concertos and 'Hungarian Dances'. He died in 1897 at 63.", "Brahms's 'Lullaby' is one of the best-known tunes in the world.", "His first symphony took him 21 years: he feared Beethoven's shadow."],
  "Любен Каравелов": ["A Bulgarian writer, journalist and revolutionary from Koprivshtitsa. In Bucharest he published newspapers and was the first chairman of the Bulgarian Revolutionary Central Committee. He died of tuberculosis in Ruse in 1879 at 44, soon after the Liberation.", "He wrote the novella 'Bulgarians of Old Times'.", "During the war he lost his library and papers in a fire in Ruse."],
  "Готлиб Даймлер": ["A German engineer and inventor from Schorndorf. With Wilhelm Maybach he built a light, fast petrol engine, the first motorcycle and one of the first cars. He founded the company that later became part of Mercedes-Benz. He died in 1900 at 65.", "The first motorcycle was made of wood and called the 'Reitwagen'.", "The Mercedes three-pointed star stands for engines on land, sea and air."],
  "Императрица Сиси": ["Elisabeth of Bavaria, wife of Emperor Franz Joseph. Famous for her beauty and long hair, she hated court life and travelled constantly. She loved Hungary and helped create Austria-Hungary. An anarchist stabbed her with a file in Geneva in 1898, at 60.", "Her hair reached her heels and washing it took a whole day.", "The wound was so thin that she kept walking without realising she was hurt."],
  "Фердинанд фон Цепелин": ["A German count and general from Konstanz. After retiring at 52 he set out to build huge rigid airships. His first Zeppelin flew over Lake Constance in 1900. He died in 1917 at 78.", "As an observer in the American Civil War he rode in a balloon for the first time.", "Zeppelins carried passengers across the Atlantic years before aeroplanes did."],
  "Хаджи Димитър": ["A Bulgarian haidut and revolutionary from Sliven. In 1868, with Stefan Karadzha, he crossed the Danube with a band of 127 men. After hard fighting he fell on Buzludzha peak at 28.", "Botev made him immortal in the poem 'Hadzhi Dimitar': 'He who falls fighting for freedom does not die…'", "His house in Sliven is now a museum."],
  "Карл Май": ["A German writer from Saxony who wrote adventures of the Wild West and the Orient, with heroes Winnetou and Old Shatterhand. As a young man he was jailed for fraud. He died in 1912 at 70.", "He wrote about America without having been there; he visited only in old age.", "His books have sold over 200 million copies."],
  "Георги Бенковски": ["A Bulgarian revolutionary from Koprivshtitsa, born Gavril Hlatev. He led the Panagyurishte revolutionary district in the April Uprising of 1876 and the 'Flying Band'. He was killed in an ambush near Teteven at 33.", "He had himself a uniform made and rode from village to village to rouse the people.", "He took the name 'Benkovski' from a Pole he met in Asia Minor."],
  "Капитан Петко войвода": ["A Bulgarian haidut and revolutionary from Doganhisar (today Aisymi, Greece). For decades he fought the Ottomans in the Rhodopes and along the Aegean. After the Liberation he lived in Varna and died in 1900 at 55.", "He fought in Italy alongside Garibaldi.", "A popular TV series, 'Captain Petko Voyvoda', was made about him."],
  "Лудвиг II Баварски": ["King of Bavaria, called the 'Fairy-Tale King'. Instead of politics he devoted himself to Wagner's music and built fairy-tale castles like Neuschwanstein. He was declared insane and deposed, and a day later found dead in Lake Starnberg at 40.", "Neuschwanstein inspired Disney's Sleeping Beauty castle.", "His death remains a mystery to this day."],
  "Ото Лилиентал": ["A German engineer, the 'Glider King'. He studied the flight of storks and made over 2,000 flights in gliders he built himself. In a crash in 1896 he broke his spine and died at 48.", "His last words are said to have been: 'Sacrifices must be made.'", "The Wright brothers studied his flights."],
  "Захари Стоянов": ["A Bulgarian revolutionary and writer from Medven, once a shepherd. He took part in the April Uprising and described it in 'Notes on the Bulgarian Uprisings'. He led the Unification of 1885 and chaired the National Assembly. He died in Paris in 1889 at 39.", "He wrote biographies of Levski, Botev and Benkovski.", "He started out as a shepherd boy and taught himself."],
  "Тодор Каблешков": ["A Bulgarian revolutionary from Koprivshtitsa. On 20 April 1876 he wrote the 'bloody letter' announcing the April Uprising. After its defeat he was captured and shot himself in custody in Gabrovo at 25.", "By tradition it is called 'bloody' because it was sealed with the blood of a killed police officer.", "He spoke several languages and studied in Constantinople."],
  "Стефан Стамболов": ["A Bulgarian revolutionary and statesman from Tarnovo. After the Liberation he was speaker of parliament and regent, then prime minister. He ruled with an iron hand but modernised the country. He was attacked with knives in Sofia and died in 1895 at 41.", "He was called 'the Bulgarian Bismarck'.", "As a young man he wrote poems and was a friend of Botev."],
  "Райна Княгиня": ["Rayna Popgeorgieva, a teacher from Panagyurishte. She sewed the flag of the April Uprising with the words 'Freedom or Death'. After the uprising she was jailed and tortured. Later she became a midwife and died in 1917 at 61.", "Benkovski gave her the nickname 'Knyaginya', 'Princess'."],
  "Княз Александър Батенберг": ["A German prince chosen in 1879, at 22, as the first prince of free Bulgaria. He backed the Unification and led the army to victory at Slivnitsa in 1885. Overthrown in a coup, he died in Graz in 1893 at 36.", "As he wished, he is buried in Sofia, where his mausoleum stands in the city centre.", "He was secretly engaged to Queen Victoria's granddaughter, but the marriage never happened."],
  "Хайнрих Херц": ["A German physicist from Hamburg. In 1887 he was the first to prove that electromagnetic waves exist, the basis of radio, TV and mobile phones. He died in 1894 at 36.", "He said his discovery was 'of no use whatsoever'.", "The unit of frequency is called the 'hertz'."],
  "Рудолф Дизел": ["A German engineer born in Paris. He invented the engine that ignites fuel just by compressing air, the diesel engine. In 1913 he vanished from a ship in the Channel and was found dead at 55.", "One of his engines ran on peanut oil at the Paris World's Fair.", "His death is still a mystery: accident, suicide or murder."],
  "Густав Малер": ["An Austrian composer and conductor from Bohemia. He ran the Vienna Opera and wrote vast symphonies full of nature, death and hope. He died in Vienna in 1911 at 50.", "He said: 'A symphony must be like the world. It must embrace everything.'", "He feared writing a ninth symphony, since Beethoven and others had died after their ninth."],
  "Цар Фердинанд": ["A German prince of Saxe-Coburg and Gotha, chosen as prince of Bulgaria in 1887. In 1908 he declared independence and became tsar. He led the country into the Balkan Wars and the First World War. He abdicated in 1918 and died in Coburg in 1948 at 87.", "A passionate botanist and entomologist, he founded gardens and museums.", "He loved driving trains himself."],
  "Петър Дънов": ["A Bulgarian spiritual teacher known as the Master Beinsa Douno. He founded the White Brotherhood and taught love, wisdom and truth. His followers dance Paneurhythmy and greet the sunrise in the Rila mountains. He died in Sofia in 1944 at 80.", "Every summer he led his followers to the Seven Rila Lakes.", "He also wrote the music for Paneurhythmy."],
  "Пенчо Славейков": ["A Bulgarian poet from Tryavna, son of Petko Slaveykov. He studied in Leipzig and wanted to raise Bulgarian poetry to European heights. He wrote the epic 'Song of Blood'. Nominated for the Nobel Prize, he died in Italy in 1912 at 46.", "As a boy he froze in icy water and was left with difficulty walking and speaking for life.", "In the anthology 'On the Isle of the Blessed' he presented invented poets whose verses were really his own."],
  "Гоце Делчев": ["A Bulgarian revolutionary and teacher from Kukush. A leader of the Internal Macedonian-Adrianople Revolutionary Organisation, which fought to free Macedonia and Thrace. He fell in a clash near the village of Banitsa in 1903 at 31.", "He said: 'I understand the world solely as a field for cultural competition among peoples.'", "He studied at the Military School in Sofia but was expelled."],
  "Яне Сандански": ["A Bulgarian revolutionary from Vlahi, called 'the Tsar of Pirin'. A voivode of the revolutionary organisation who led bands in the Pirin mountains. He organised the kidnapping of the American missionary Miss Stone for ransom. He was killed in an ambush near Rozhen in 1915 at 42.", "The ransom for Miss Stone was 14,000 Turkish pounds, raised in America.", "The town of Sandanski bears his name."],
  "Фердинанд Порше": ["An Austrian car designer from Bohemia. He built one of the first hybrid cars back in 1900, and in the 1930s designed the Volkswagen Beetle. He founded the Porsche company and died in 1951 at 75.", "Over 21 million Beetles were built.", "He never finished university but received an honorary doctorate."],
  "Райнер Мария Рилке": ["An Austrian poet born in Prague. He wrote the 'Duino Elegies' and 'Sonnets to Orpheus', and his 'Letters to a Young Poet' are still read today. He died of leukaemia in Switzerland in 1926 at 51.", "As a little boy his mother dressed him as a girl.", "He worked as secretary to the sculptor Rodin in Paris."],
  "Конрад Аденауер": ["A German politician and mayor of Cologne removed by the Nazis. After the war, at 73, he became West Germany's first chancellor and led it for 14 years, to democracy, an economic miracle and reconciliation with France. He died in 1967 at 91.", "He was elected chancellor by a majority of one vote: his own.", "He patented a soy sausage and a device against insects."],
  "Херман Хесе": ["A German writer from the Black Forest, later a Swiss citizen. In novels like 'Steppenwolf', 'Siddhartha' and 'The Glass Bead Game' he explored the search for oneself. He won the Nobel Prize in 1946 and died in 1962 at 85.", "As a boy he ran away from a seminary school.", "In the 1960s he became a favourite of the hippies."],
  "Стамен Григоров": ["A Bulgarian doctor and microbiologist from the village of Studen Izvor. In 1905 in Geneva he discovered the bacterium that makes Bulgarian yoghurt, Lactobacillus bulgaricus. Later he also made a tuberculosis vaccine. He died in 1945 at 67.", "He found it at 27, while still a student.", "Studen Izvor has a museum of yoghurt."],
  "Лизе Майтнер": ["An Austrian physicist from Vienna. She worked for 30 years in Berlin with Otto Hahn. Being Jewish, she fled the Nazis to Sweden, from where she explained nuclear fission. She refused to work on the atomic bomb. She died in England in 1968 at 89.", "Only Hahn received the Nobel Prize for fission; she was passed over.", "The element meitnerium is named after her."],
  "Александър Стамболийски": ["A Bulgarian politician from Slavovitsa, leader of the Agrarian Union. Opposed to the war, he was jailed, and after it became prime minister. He carried out land reform and introduced compulsory labour service. Overthrown in a coup, he was brutally killed in 1923 at 44.", "His killers cut off the hand he had used to sign the treaty with Yugoslavia."],
  "Ото Хан": ["A German chemist from Frankfurt, 'father of nuclear chemistry'. In 1938, with Fritz Strassmann, he discovered the splitting of the uranium nucleus. He received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry. After the war he campaigned against nuclear weapons. He died in 1968 at 89.", "When he heard about Hiroshima he was so distressed that his colleagues feared for him.", "He learned of his Nobel Prize while held by the British."],
  "Йордан Йовков": ["A Bulgarian writer from Zheravna, a master of the short story. A teacher in Dobrudja, then a diplomat in Bucharest. He wrote 'Legends of the Balkan Mountains', 'Last Joy' and 'The Farm at the Border'. He died in Plovdiv in 1937 at 56.", "His story 'The Song of the Wheels' is read in schools.", "He fought as an officer in the Balkan Wars and the First World War."],
  "Алфред Вегенер": ["A German meteorologist and polar explorer from Berlin. He proposed that the continents were once one, Pangaea, and slowly drift apart. Nobody believed him in his lifetime. He died in Greenland in 1930 at 50, during an expedition.", "He noticed that South America's coast fits Africa like a jigsaw piece.", "He set a world record for the longest balloon flight: over 52 hours."],
  "Стефан Цвайг": ["An Austrian writer from Vienna, one of the most read in the world in the 1930s. He wrote novellas, biographies and the memoir 'The World of Yesterday'. Being Jewish, he fled the Nazis to England and then Brazil, where he took his own life in 1942 at 60.", "His last novella is 'The Royal Game' (Chess Story).", "The Nazis burned his books."],
  "Владимир Димитров – Майстора": ["A Bulgarian painter from Frolosh near Kyustendil. He painted villagers, maidens and fruit in bright, fairy-tale colours. He lived simply in the village of Shishkovtsi. He died in 1960 at 78.", "He gave his paintings to the villagers who fed him.", "He was called 'the Master' from his youth."],
  "Асен Златаров": ["A Bulgarian chemist, doctor and public figure from Haskovo. He studied the chemistry of food and wrote popular science books. He organised free lectures and help for the poor. He died in 1936 at 51.", "He also wrote poetry.", "The university in Burgas bears his name."],
  "Ран Босилек": ["Gencho Negentsov, a Bulgarian children's writer from Gabrovo. He wrote 'Patilantsi' and 'Unborn Maiden', and translated and retold fairy tales. He died in 1958 at 71.", "His pen name means 'early basil'.", "The heroes of 'Patilantsi', Rizhko, Gogo and Sachko, are known to every Bulgarian child."],
  "Дора Габе": ["A Bulgarian poet from Dabovik near Dobrich. She wrote poems for children and adults and translated from many languages. She lived almost a century and died in 1983 at 97.", "She studied in Geneva and Grenoble and spoke several languages.", "Generations of children know her poems by heart."],
  "Димчо Дебелянов": ["A Bulgarian poet from Koprivshtitsa. He wrote gentle, sad poems such as 'To Return to Your Father's House'. He volunteered in the First World War and fell near Gorno Karadzhovo in 1916 at 29.", "His poem 'I Want to Remember You Like This' is one of the most loved in Bulgaria.", "The statue on his grave in Koprivshtitsa, a mother waiting for her son, is known throughout the country."],
  "Ервин Шрьодингер": ["An Austrian physicist from Vienna, one of the creators of quantum mechanics. His equation describes how electrons behave. He won the Nobel Prize in 1933 and died in Vienna in 1961 at 73.", "He came up with the thought experiment of a cat that is both alive and dead until you open the box.", "His face was on the Austrian 1,000-schilling banknote."],
  "Чудомир": ["Dimitar Chorbadzhiyski, a Bulgarian humorist writer and painter from Turia. A teacher in Kazanlak, he wrote funny, warm stories about village life. He died in 1967 at 76.", "He signed simply as 'Chudomir'.", "His house in Kazanlak is a museum that hosts a festival of humour."],
  "Дан Колов": ["A Bulgarian wrestler from Sennik near Sevlievo, a world freestyle champion. At 17 he went to America, worked as a lumberjack and miner, then became a ring star. He lost only a handful of more than 1,500 bouts. He died in his home village in 1940 at 47.", "He said: 'A Bulgarian cannot be beaten!'", "He reportedly held back two cars pulling in opposite directions."],
  "Цар Борис III": ["Tsar of Bulgaria from 1918 to 1943, son of Ferdinand. He ruled in hard years and in the Second World War was Germany's ally but sent no troops to the Eastern Front. He died suddenly after a meeting with Hitler in 1943 at 49.", "Like his father, he loved trains and drove locomotives himself.", "During his reign the Jews of Bulgaria proper were saved from deportation."],
  "Димитър Пешев": ["A Bulgarian politician from Kyustendil, deputy speaker of parliament. In 1943 he learned of the planned deportation of Bulgaria's Jews and stopped it by collecting MPs' signatures. He lost his post and was later tried by the communists. He died in poverty in 1973 at 78.", "Israel named him 'Righteous Among the Nations'.", "His actions saved about 48,000 people."],
  "Гео Милев": ["A Bulgarian avant-garde poet from Radnevo. Wounded in the First World War, he lost his right eye. His poem 'September' described the crushed uprising of 1923 and was banned. In 1925 he was taken in by the police and disappeared; he was killed at 30.", "His remains were found only in 1954, in a mass grave.", "He studied in Leipzig and translated European poets."],
  "Георги Наджаков": ["A Bulgarian physicist from Dupnitsa. In 1937 he discovered the photoelectret effect, later the basis of photocopiers and laser printers. He was a professor at Sofia University. He died in 1981 at 84.", "He worked with Paul Langevin in Paris.", "Without his discovery, copy machines might have come much later."],
  "Иван Странски": ["A Bulgarian physical chemist from Sofia, founder of the science of crystal growth. He worked in Sofia and Berlin, where he was rector of the Technical University. He died in Berlin in 1979 at 82.", "The 'Stranski–Krastanov' growth mode is still used today in making chips."],
  "Иван Милев": ["A Bulgarian painter from Kazanlak. In a short life he created fairy-tale paintings drawing on folklore, legends and Art Nouveau. He died of Spanish flu in 1927 at 30.", "His face is on the 5-lev banknote.", "He also painted theatre sets."],
  "Христо Смирненски": ["A Bulgarian poet from Kukush. He wrote about the poor and oppressed, as well as humorous verse. He died of tuberculosis in 1923 at 24.", "His 'Tale of the Staircase' is read in every school.", "As a boy he lost two fingers playing with explosives."],
  "Димитър Талев": ["A Bulgarian writer from Prilep. He wrote the novel 'The Iron Candlestick' and its sequels about life in Macedonia during the Revival. The communists sent him to a labour camp. He died in 1966 at 68.", "'The Iron Candlestick' is among the most loved Bulgarian novels.", "He was nominated for the Nobel Prize."],
  "Бертолт Брехт": ["A German playwright and poet from Augsburg. He wrote 'The Threepenny Opera' and 'Mother Courage' and created 'epic theatre', which makes the audience think, not only feel. He fled the Nazis and later founded the Berliner Ensemble. He died in 1956 at 58.", "'Mack the Knife' from his opera became a worldwide hit.", "In America he was questioned about communist views, and left the next day."],
  "Ерих Мария Ремарк": ["A German writer from Osnabrück. Wounded at 18 in the First World War, in 1929 he wrote 'All Quiet on the Western Front', one of the most famous anti-war novels. The Nazis burned his books and he emigrated. He died in Switzerland in 1970 at 72.", "His sister was executed by the Nazis, partly because of him.", "He was married to the Hollywood actress Paulette Goddard."],
  "Панчо Владигеров": ["A Bulgarian composer and pianist, born in Zurich. He studied and worked in Berlin, then returned to Sofia and taught at the Music Academy. He wrote the 'Bulgarian Rhapsody Vardar'. He died in 1978 at 79.", "He had a twin brother, Lyuben, a violinist.", "The Music Academy in Sofia bears his name."],
  "Ерих Кестнер": ["A German writer from Dresden. He wrote the children's books 'Emil and the Detectives', 'Lottie and Lisa' and 'The Flying Classroom'. The Nazis burned his books while he watched. He died in Munich in 1974 at 75.", "He was the only author to watch his own books burn in 1933.", "'The Parent Trap' was filmed from 'Lottie and Lisa'."],
  "Илия Бешков": ["A Bulgarian artist and cartoonist from Pleven. His cartoons mocked those in power and war. He taught at the Academy of Fine Arts. He died in 1958 at 57.", "He also wrote stories and memoirs."],
  "Вернер Хайзенберг": ["A German physicist from Würzburg, a creator of quantum mechanics. He found the 'uncertainty principle': you cannot know both the exact position and speed of a particle. He won the Nobel Prize at 31. He died in 1976 at 74.", "During the war he led the German nuclear programme, which never got to a bomb.", "He worked out a key part of his theory on the island of Heligoland, where he had gone because of hay fever."],
  "Ангел Каралийчев": ["A Bulgarian writer from Strazhitsa, author of fairy tales and stories for children. He wrote 'Rye Grain' and 'The Wheat Loaf'. He died in 1972 at 69.", "His tales have been translated into dozens of languages.", "Many of his tales are based on folk stories."],
  "Хайнц Рюман": ["A German actor from Essen, one of the most loved in the history of German cinema. He appeared in over 100 films, comedies like 'Die Feuerzangenbowle' and serious roles. He died in 1994 at 92.", "A keen pilot, he flew planes into his old age.", "At 91 he appeared in a film by Wim Wenders."],
  "Джон Атанасов": ["An American physicist and inventor whose father was Bulgarian, from Boychinovtsi. In 1939–1942, with Clifford Berry, he built the first electronic digital computer. He died in 1995 at 91.", "The idea came to him in a roadside tavern after driving some 300 km through the night.", "In 1973 a US court ruled that he had invented the electronic computer."],
  "Златю Бояджиев": ["A Bulgarian painter from Brezovo. He painted village scenes, festivals and Plovdiv streets. After a stroke at 48 he learned to paint with his left hand and created fairy-tale paintings crowded with people. He died in 1976 at 72.", "His paintings after the stroke are full of hundreds of tiny figures.", "His museum is in Plovdiv's Old Town."],
  "Филип Кутев": ["A Bulgarian composer and conductor from Aytos. He founded the State Ensemble for Folk Songs and Dances, which made Bulgarian folk music known worldwide. He died in 1982 at 79.", "His ensemble was the first professional folk ensemble in Bulgaria (1951).", "His wife Maria Kuteva worked with him and carried on his work."],
  "Макс Шмелинг": ["A German boxer from Brandenburg, world heavyweight champion. In 1936 he knocked out the unbeaten American Joe Louis. Later he became a businessman and Louis's friend. He died in 2005 at 99.", "In 1938 he hid two Jewish boys in his hotel room and saved them.", "He helped pay for Joe Louis's funeral."],
  "Хана Арент": ["A German-American philosopher from a Jewish family in Hanover. She fled the Nazis to France and then the USA. She wrote about totalitarianism and the 'banality of evil'. She died in New York in 1975 at 69.", "At Eichmann's trial she saw not a monster but an ordinary bureaucrat.", "She was a student of the philosopher Martin Heidegger."],
  "Емилиян Станев": ["A Bulgarian writer from Tarnovo. He wrote the novel 'Antichrist' and stories about nature and animals, as well as 'The Peach Thief'. A keen hunter, he died in 1979 at 72.", "'The Peach Thief' was made into a film.", "He loved roaming the forests alone."],
  "Димитър Димов": ["A Bulgarian writer and veterinarian from Lovech. He wrote the novels 'Tobacco' and 'Doomed Souls'. A professor of anatomy, he died suddenly in Bucharest in 1966 at 56.", "'Doomed Souls' is set in Spain during the Civil War.", "He had to rewrite 'Tobacco' at the authorities' demand."],
  "Вили Бранд": ["A German politician from Lübeck, born Herbert Frahm. He fled the Nazis to Norway, later became mayor of West Berlin and chancellor of West Germany. He sought reconciliation with Eastern Europe and won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1971. He died in 1992 at 78.", "In 1970 in Warsaw he knelt before the memorial to the ghetto victims, a gesture seen around the world.", "He resigned after one of his aides turned out to be an East German spy."],
  "Борис Христов": ["A Bulgarian operatic bass from Plovdiv. He sang on the world's greatest stages and was famous as Boris Godunov. He lived in Italy and died in Rome in 1993 at 79. He is buried in Sofia.", "He sang in the choir of the Alexander Nevsky Cathedral before becoming a world star.", "He recorded all of Mussorgsky's songs."],
  "Валери Петров": ["A Bulgarian poet, playwright and translator from Sofia. He wrote the screenplay of 'Knight Without Armour' and the 'White Tale', and translated all of Shakespeare's plays. He died in 2014 at 94.", "He translated all of Shakespeare's plays on his own.", "Songs with his lyrics are still sung today."],
  "Блага Димитрова": ["A Bulgarian poet and novelist from Byala Slatina. She wrote poems and novels, and after 1989 became Vice President of Bulgaria. She died in 2003 at 81.", "During the Vietnam War she adopted a Vietnamese girl.", "Her novel 'Avalanche' was made into a film."],
  "Апостол Карамитев": ["A Bulgarian actor from Dupnitsa. He played on stage and in films. He taught acting to generations of actors. He died in 1973 at 50.", "His son Momchil Karamitev is also an actor."],
  "Георги Калоянчев": ["A Bulgarian actor from Burgas, 'Kalata', loved by several generations. He played comic and dramatic roles on stage and screen. He died in 2012 at 87.", "He was called 'the king of comedy'."],
  "Гюнтер Грас": ["A German writer from Danzig. In 1959 he wrote 'The Tin Drum', about a boy who refuses to grow. He won the Nobel Prize in 1999 and died in 2015 at 87.", "At 17 he had served in the Waffen-SS, which he admitted only at 78.", "Besides writing he was a sculptor and painter."],
  "Николай Гяуров": ["A Bulgarian operatic bass from Velingrad. He sang at La Scala, the Met and Vienna and is counted among the greatest basses. He died in Modena in 2004 at 74.", "He was married to the Italian soprano Mirella Freni."],
  "Михаел Енде": ["A German writer from Garmisch. He wrote 'Momo', 'The Neverending Story' and 'Jim Button and Luke the Engine Driver'. He died in 1995 at 65.", "'The Neverending Story' is printed in two colours of ink, one for each world.", "He disliked the film of his book and sued the producers."],
  "Хелмут Кол": ["A German politician from Ludwigshafen. Chancellor for 16 years, the longest since Bismarck. In 1990 he reunited Germany and backed the creation of the euro. He died in 2017 at 87.", "He is called 'the chancellor of unity'.", "He liked to treat foreign guests to stuffed pig's stomach in his home region, the Palatinate."],
  "Христо Явашев – Кристо": ["A Bulgarian artist from Gabrovo, known worldwide as Christo. With his wife Jeanne-Claude he wrapped buildings and landscapes: the Reichstag in Berlin, the Pont Neuf in Paris. He died in New York in 2020 at 84.", "He and Jeanne-Claude were born on the same day, 13 June 1935.", "He paid for all his projects himself by selling sketches."],
  "Невена Коканова": ["A Bulgarian actress from Dupnitsa, one of the most loved in film. She starred in films like 'The Iconostasis' and 'The Prince'. She died in 2000 at 61.", "She appeared in over 30 films.", "She started in film without any acting training."],
  "Роми Шнайдер": ["An Austrian-German actress from Vienna. She became a star at 17 playing Empress Sisi, then made many strong films in France. After her son's death she died in 1982 at 43.", "She came to hate the role of Sisi because everyone tied her to it.", "She was engaged to Alain Delon."],
  "Тодор Колев": ["A Bulgarian actor, showman and singer from Shumen. He starred in comedies and hosted popular TV shows. He died in 2013 at 74.", "He was nicknamed 'the Great'.", "He also sang his own songs."],
  "Емил Димитров": ["A Bulgarian singer and composer from Plovdiv, a pop star. He wrote and sang 'My Country, My Bulgaria'. He died in 2005 at 64.", "'My Country, My Bulgaria' is almost a second anthem.", "His records sold in the millions in the Soviet Union too."],
  "Гена Димитрова": ["A Bulgarian operatic soprano from Beykovo. She sang on the greatest stages, La Scala, the Met, the Verona Arena, and was famous as Turandot. She died in 2005 at 64.", "She was called 'the voice of the century'.", "Turandot was her signature role around the world."],
  "Стефан Данаилов": ["A Bulgarian actor from Sofia, a public favourite. He appeared in over 100 films, including 'At Every Kilometre', and in Italian films. Later he was Minister of Culture. He died in 2019 at 76.", "The role of Deyanov in 'At Every Kilometre' made him a star.", "He appeared in the Italian series 'La Piovra'."],
  "Георги Аспарухов – Гунди": ["A Bulgarian footballer from Sofia, a Levski legend. A graceful goal scorer, he played at three World Cups. He died in a car crash in 1971 at 28.", "Around 550,000 people came to his funeral.", "He turned down an offer from AC Milan to stay at Levski."],
  "Франц Бекенбауер": ["A German footballer from Munich, 'Der Kaiser'. Captain of Germany, world champion as a player in 1974 and as coach in 1990. He invented the modern sweeper role. He died in 2024 at 78.", "He played a World Cup semi-final with a dislocated shoulder in a sling.", "As a boy he wanted to join 1860 Munich, but after one of their players slapped him he went to Bayern."],
  "Фалко": ["An Austrian pop singer from Vienna, born Johann Hölzel. His 'Rock Me Amadeus' was the first German-language song to top the US charts. He died in a car crash in the Dominican Republic in 1998 at 40.", "He had perfect pitch even as a child.", "'Der Kommissar' was a worldwide hit."],
  "Петя Дубарова": ["A Bulgarian poet from Burgas. She wrote fresh, sincere poems while still at school and acted in a film. She took her own life in 1979 at 17.", "Most of her poems were published after her death.", "Her house in Burgas is a museum."],
};
